Guide.content._register("w00", {"id":"w00","order":0,"region":"foundations","title":"Orientation","short":"Orientation","tagline":"A question is typed into a box. This is where the journey, the method and the map begin.","story":"A question is typed into a box and starts a journey. It will be counted, cached, matched with knowledge, checked, routed through tools and agents, and judged on the way out. At every stop something breaks or forces a choice, and the best answer depends on dials that nobody has told you yet.","oneLiner":"Learn how the guide plays, practise Dial-and-Defend on a gentle board, and read a dated map of what changed in 2025 and 2026 before you quote a spec.","youWillLearn":["Trace one question through the journey and name the world where each failure is fixed","Use the level loop: brief, options, dials, failure modes, quiz, defend, flashcards and references","Answer with Dial-and-Defend: clarify, list options, pick and flip, failure modes, metrics and rollout","Rank four ways to ground an assistant (prompt, RAG, tools, fine-tuning) as constraints change","Read a dated freshness map of 2025 and 2026 changes before you quote a spec in an answer","Run a workshop with facilitator mode, and export and import progress"],"minutes":85,"prereqs":[],"flashcards":[{"id":"w00-fc-001","front":"What are the five moves of Dial-and-Defend?","back":"1 Clarify the dials. 2 List the options, including the boring one. 3 Pick for the stated dials and say what flips it. 4 Name failure modes and detection. 5 Name metrics and rollout.","tags":["method"]},{"id":"w00-fc-002","front":"Why ask about dials before naming a technology?","back":"The best option moves with corpus size, update rate, permissions, latency, risk and cost. Interviewers listen for the dial set first.","tags":["method"]},{"id":"w00-fc-003","front":"What is a dial?","back":"A constraint you can change, such as scale or permission model, that moves which option wins.","tags":["method"]},{"id":"w00-fc-004","front":"What is the \"boring option\" in step 2?","back":"The simplest approach that could work, such as pasting the content into a cached prompt. It is the baseline every other option must beat.","tags":["method"]},{"id":"w00-fc-005","front":"What does a good flip condition sound like?","back":"It names a dial and a threshold: \"If the corpus outgrows the window, or permissions become per user, I switch to RAG.\"","tags":["method"]},{"id":"w00-fc-006","front":"What steps make up one problem level?","back":"Brief, options board, flip the dials, failure modes, quiz, defend it, flashcards and references.","tags":["guide"]},{"id":"w00-fc-007","front":"Where does the guide save your progress?","back":"In your browser's local storage on that computer. Export a file from the footer or the Progress page to back it up or move it.","tags":["guide"]},{"id":"w00-fc-008","front":"What happens to progress if you clear site data or switch browsers?","back":"It is lost unless you exported first. Import the file to merge with or replace existing progress.","tags":["guide"]},{"id":"w00-fc-009","front":"What does facilitator mode do?","back":"It reveals quiz answers and model answers with an Answer key badge, and includes them when you print a level. Keep it off on learners' screens.","tags":["guide"]},{"id":"w00-fc-010","front":"How do you flip and grade a flashcard from the keyboard?","back":"Space flips the card. Keys 1 to 4 grade it.","tags":["guide"]},{"id":"w00-fc-011","front":"When did OpenAI release the Responses API, and when did the Assistants API shut down?","back":"Responses on 2025-03-11. Assistants deprecation was announced 2025-08-26 and the API shut down on 2026-08-26.","tags":["freshness","api"]},{"id":"w00-fc-012","front":"What changed for prompt caching with the GPT-5.6 family?","back":"Cache writes bill at 1.25x input, explicit breakpoints and `prompt_cache_options` appear, `prompt_cache_retention` is deprecated and `prompt_cache_key` is optional. Older models return 400 for the new fields.","tags":["freshness","caching"]},{"id":"w00-fc-013","front":"What changed in the MCP revision dated 2026-07-28?","back":"A stateless protocol (no `initialize`, no session id), multi round-trip requests, Tasks moved to an extension, and sampling, roots, logging and DCR deprecated.","tags":["freshness","mcp"]},{"id":"w00-fc-014","front":"Which A2A versions matter, and which does Foundry serve by default?","back":"v1.0.0 (2026-03-12) and v1.0.1 (2026-05-28). With no version header or `a2a-version` query value, Foundry serves preview 0.3.","tags":["freshness","a2a"]},{"id":"w00-fc-015","front":"When did Microsoft Agent Framework reach 1.0?","back":"Announced 2026-04-03 for .NET and Python. Stable: agents, graph workflows, orchestrations, MCP. Preview at GA: memory, DevUI, hosted agent integration, AG-UI and more.","tags":["freshness","agents"]},{"id":"w00-fc-016","front":"When did Agent 365 reach GA, and for whom?","back":"2026-05-01, for the Commercial segment, licensed per user. At least one user needs a qualifying license.","tags":["freshness","agents"]},{"id":"w00-fc-017","front":"What ended on 2026-08-20 for Foundry hosted agents?","back":"Support for the initial-preview hosted agent backend. Existing deployments are not migrated automatically, so redeploy on the new model.","tags":["freshness","agents"]},{"id":"w00-fc-018","front":"Which dates should you pin before the end of 2026?","back":"2026-10-23 OpenAI model shutdowns, 2026-11-19 Azure o-series retirements, 2026-11-30 Evals and prompts shutdown, 2026-12-01 Foundry Workflows retire, 2026-12-09 Azure gpt-4o 2024-05-13 retires.","tags":["freshness"]},{"id":"w00-fc-019","front":"Why label every product fact with a date and a status?","back":"Vendors change names, defaults and availability within months. A fact without a date and a GA or preview label cannot be checked.","tags":["freshness","method"]},{"id":"w00-fc-020","front":"What is a good study loop for one world?","back":"Read the brief, argue the dials aloud, take the quiz, write the defend answer from memory, then review the flashcards the next day.","tags":["guide"]},{"id":"w00-t01-fc1","front":"What is the main unit of this guide?","back":"The topic: a standalone page that explains, says where and when to use it, compares alternatives, lists pitfalls and offers scenario cards.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t01"},{"id":"w00-t01-fc2","front":"How do you keep progress across browsers?","back":"Export the progress JSON from the Progress page and import it elsewhere, using merge to keep the best of both files.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t01"},{"id":"w00-t01-fc3","front":"What does facilitator mode do?","back":"It reveals quiz and model answers with an Answer key badge. Enable it only on the host's screen.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t01"},{"id":"w00-t02-fc1","front":"Name the nine stops a question passes through.","back":"Budget and API, latency and cache, knowledge, tools and protocols, agents, then senses, proof and operations around the answer.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t02"},{"id":"w00-t02-fc2","front":"What is the default rule for how much of the stack to use?","back":"Use the simplest layer that meets the constraints, and add retrieval, tools or agents only when a measured gap appears.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t02"},{"id":"w00-t02-fc3","front":"Where does access control get decided?","back":"At ingestion, when ACLs are captured, and at query time through filters. It is not a late add-on.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t02"},{"id":"w00-t03-fc1","front":"List the five moves of Dial-and-Defend.","back":"Clarify the dials, list the options (with the boring baseline), pick and flip, name failure modes with detection, then metrics and rollout.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t03"},{"id":"w00-t03-fc2","front":"What makes a flip condition good?","back":"It names a dial, a threshold and the option you switch to, for example: if permissions become per user, I move to RAG.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t03"},{"id":"w00-t03-fc3","front":"Name the seven dials in the checklist.","back":"Corpus, update rate, permissions, tenancy, latency, risk and cost.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t03"},{"id":"w00-t04-fc1","front":"What do the constraints on a scenario card tell you?","back":"Which dials are fixed in this card. Facts not listed are open, and the unknowns name the questions you would ask.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t04"},{"id":"w00-t04-fc2","front":"How do you use a poor option when revising?","back":"Read where it would be right. That sentence is a flip condition you can say in an interview.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t04"},{"id":"w00-t04-fc3","front":"Two options both fit every constraint. What do you do?","back":"Pick the cheaper one to reverse and name the unknown that would flip you to the other.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t04"},{"id":"w00-t05-fc1","front":"What happened to the OpenAI Assistants API?","back":"Deprecation was announced on 2025-08-26 and the API was sunset on 2026-08-26. Threads map to Conversations and Runs to Responses.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t05"},{"id":"w00-t05-fc2","front":"What changed in MCP on 2026-07-28?","back":"The protocol became stateless: no `initialize` and no session id, multi round-trip requests replaced server push, and Tasks moved to an extension.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t05"},{"id":"w00-t05-fc3","front":"Why can an unset reasoning effort still break tool calls?","back":"The model applies its default, often `medium`, and Chat Completions rejects tools with any effort other than `none` on newer models.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t05"},{"id":"w00-t05-fc4","front":"Why name the platform when quoting a retirement date?","back":"The same model can retire on different dates on OpenAI and on Azure.","tags":[],"topic":"w00-t05"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"OpenAI API changelog","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/changelog","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Dated API, model and feature releases used for the freshness map.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r2","title":"OpenAI deprecations","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/deprecations","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Shutdown dates for models, Evals, prompts, Agent Builder and fine-tuning.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r3","title":"Migrate to the Responses API","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/migrate-to-responses","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Why Responses is recommended and where Chat Completions falls short with tools and reasoning.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r4","title":"Assistants migration guide","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/assistants/migration","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"The Assistants API sunset and the mapping to Conversations and Responses.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r5","title":"Prompt caching (OpenAI)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"GPT-5.6 explicit breakpoints, write charges and options.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r6","title":"Prompt caching in Azure OpenAI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Azure retention defaults and the GPT-5.6 cache options.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r7","title":"Claude Platform release notes","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/release-notes/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Dated model launches and breaking changes for Claude.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r8","title":"MCP specification changelog, 2026-07-28","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/changelog","type":"spec","publisher":"Model Context Protocol","note":"The stateless model, multi round-trip requests and deprecations.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r9","title":"MCP joins the Agentic AI Foundation","url":"https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2025-12-09-mcp-joins-agentic-ai-foundation/","type":"blog","publisher":"Model Context Protocol","note":"The 2025-12-09 governance change.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r10","title":"What's new in A2A v1.0","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/whats-new-v1/","type":"doc","publisher":"A2A Project","note":"The migration list from 0.3 to 1.0.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r11","title":"Enable incoming A2A on a Foundry agent","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/enable-agent-to-agent-endpoint","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Version selection, defaults and limitations on Foundry.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r12","title":"Microsoft Agent Framework version 1.0","url":"https://devblogs.microsoft.com/agent-framework/microsoft-agent-framework-version-1-0/","type":"blog","publisher":"Microsoft","note":"What was stable and what was preview at 1.0.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r13","title":"Hosted agents in Foundry Agent Service","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/concepts/hosted-agents","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Sessions, protocols and identity for hosted agents. No explicit GA statement was found.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r14","title":"What's new in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/whats-new-foundry","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Monthly updates, including the long-running agent articles marked preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r15","title":"Microsoft Agent 365 overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-agent-365/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Observe, govern and secure pillars, availability and licensing.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r16","title":"Azure AI Search REST API versions","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/searchservice/search-service-api-versions","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Stable and preview data-plane versions and what each carries.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r17","title":"Define index projections in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-how-to-define-index-projections","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Index projections on the stable API.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r18","title":"Azure OpenAI model retirement schedule","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/model-retirement-schedule","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Per-model retirement dates on Azure.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r19","title":"Azure OpenAI On Your Data (classic)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry-classic/openai/concepts/use-your-data","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Retirement on 2026-10-14 and the migration path.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"deprecated"},{"id":"r20","title":"Azure Content Understanding overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/ai-services/content-understanding/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"GA API version and the later preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r21","title":"OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/2025/12/09/owasp-top-10-for-agentic-applications-the-benchmark-for-agentic-security-in-the-age-of-autonomous-ai/","type":"blog","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"ASI01 to ASI10 with real incidents.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r22","title":"AI Omnibus enters into force","url":"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/ai-omnibus-enters-force","type":"doc","publisher":"European Commission","note":"New application dates for Annex III and Annex I high-risk rules.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r23","title":"Introduction to generative AI and agents","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/training/modules/fundamentals-generative-ai/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Study material: shared vocabulary before the dials.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r24","title":"Intro to Large Language Models","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zjkBMFhNj_g","type":"video","publisher":"Andrej Karpathy","note":"Study material: a one-hour mental model of what an LLM is.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r25","title":"General availability of Microsoft Foundry features","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/general-availability","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The GA and preview status of each Foundry capability.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r26","title":"Introducing Contextual Retrieval","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"The 200,000 token rule of thumb for putting a knowledge base in the prompt.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r27","title":"Fine-Tuning or Retrieval? Comparing Knowledge Injection in LLMs","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.05934","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"RAG beat unsupervised fine-tuning for knowledge.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r28","title":"Azure OpenAI v1 API and API version lifecycle","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/api-version-lifecycle","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The /openai/v1 base URL and no required api-version.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r29","title":"Data controls in the OpenAI platform","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/your-data","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"ZDR behaviour and which endpoints are eligible.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r30","title":"Using GPT-6","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/latest-model","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"GPT-6 migration rules for effort, async tools and cache options.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r31","title":"Flex processing in Azure OpenAI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/flex-processing","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Flex behaviour, including the 400 on unsupported models.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r32","title":"A2A Protocol specification","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/","type":"spec","publisher":"A2A Project","note":"Normative source for the protocol, including the v1.0.1 patch note.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r33","title":"Migrate a hosted agent from the initial preview backend","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/agents/how-to/migrate-hosted-agent-preview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Support for the initial-preview backend ends on 2026-08-20.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"deprecated"},{"id":"tr1","title":"Building effective agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Defines workflows and agents and advises finding the simplest solution before adding complexity.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr2","title":"MCP specification changelog, 2025-03-26","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-03-26/changelog","type":"spec","publisher":"Model Context Protocol","note":"Streamable HTTP replaces HTTP+SSE and the OAuth 2.1 framework arrives.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr3","title":"MCP specification changelog, 2025-06-18","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/changelog","type":"spec","publisher":"Model Context Protocol","note":"Servers become OAuth resource servers and batching is removed.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr4","title":"MCP specification changelog, 2025-11-25","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/changelog","type":"spec","publisher":"Model Context Protocol","note":"URL elicitation and experimental Tasks.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr5","title":"Azure OpenAI reasoning models","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/reasoning","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Reasoning feature tables and the tools-with-reasoning limitation on Azure.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Dial","def":"A constraint you can change, such as scale, latency or permission model, that moves which option wins."},{"term":"Dial-and-Defend","def":"The five-move answer method: clarify the dials, list options, pick and flip, name failure modes, name metrics and rollout."},{"term":"Flip condition","def":"The dial and threshold at which your chosen option stops being the best one."},{"term":"Boring option","def":"The simplest approach that could work. It is the baseline every other option must beat."},{"term":"Failure mode","def":"A named way a design breaks, with the symptom you would see and the signal you would use to detect it."},{"term":"Boss level","def":"The last level of a world. It poses an interview-style question with unspecified dials and scores your answer against a rubric."},{"term":"Freshness map","def":"A dated list of changes with the source and status of each, used to check a fact before quoting it."},{"term":"GA","def":"Generally available. The vendor supports the feature for production use under its normal terms."},{"term":"Preview","def":"Available for early use, usually without an SLA, and likely to change. Label it as preview in an answer."},{"term":"Sunset","def":"The date a vendor stops serving an API or model. After it, calls fail, so plan the migration before the date."},{"term":"Topic","def":"A standalone page in this guide: mechanism, where and when to use it, alternatives, pitfalls, scenario cards, quick checks and flashcards."},{"term":"Scenario card","def":"A short situation with fixed constraints, open unknowns and options that each carry a verdict, a reason and a trade-off."},{"term":"Verdict","def":"The label on a scenario option: **best** fits every stated constraint, **good** is acceptable with a cost, **poor** fails a constraint or adds cost the constraints do not need."},{"term":"Facilitator mode","def":"A setting that shows quiz and model answers with an Answer key badge. Turn it on only on the host's screen in a group session."},{"term":"Leitner box","def":"One of five review levels for flashcards. A card moves up when you recall it and back when you do not."},{"term":"System map","def":"The stack a question travels through, from the model call to knowledge, tools, agents, proof and operations, used to place a problem on a layer."},{"term":"Deprecated","def":"A feature or model that still works but has an announced end date."},{"term":"Retired","def":"A feature or model that has stopped working after its end date."}],"levels":[{"id":"w00-l1","order":1,"title":"A question enters the box","kind":"concept","minutes":10,"brief":"A user types one question into a box. Before it becomes an answer it will be counted, cached, matched with knowledge, checked and routed. Find the stop where each kind of failure belongs.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The journey in one picture","level":2,"id":"the-journey-in-one-picture"},{"t":"p","md":"This guide follows **one question** through an AI system. Each stop on the way is a world. At every stop something breaks or forces a choice, and you solve it by weighing options against dials such as corpus size, update rate, permission model, latency, risk and cost. You never learn one fixed scenario, because the right answer moves when a dial moves."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w00-d-journey","title":"The journey of one question","alt":"A question passes through model and API, latency and cache, knowledge, tools and protocols, agents and the answer. Then senses, evaluation and observability, operations and governance, and finally boss rounds.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Question] --> M[w01 Model and API]\n  M --> L[w02 Latency and cache]\n  L --> K[w03 to w07 Knowledge]\n  K --> T[w08 to w09 Tools and protocols]\n  T --> A[w10 to w12 Agents]\n  A --> R[Answer]\n  R --> S[w13 to w15 Senses and proof]\n  S --> O[w16 to w18 Operate and govern]\n  O --> B[w19 Boss rounds]","caption":"World 0 is this orientation. It sits before the question is typed."},{"t":"table","caption":"Six regions, twenty worlds","head":["Region","Worlds","What breaks","You can answer afterwards"],"rows":[["Foundations","w00 to w02","Token budgets, rejected parameters, the wrong API, slow first tokens, cold caches","Which knob, which API, and why is it slow?"],["Knowledge","w03 to w07","Content never ingested, split badly, retrieved wrongly, unsafe or leaking","How does the right, permitted content reach the model?"],["Agents and protocols","w08 to w12","Tool misuse, protocol mismatch, agent loops, the wrong runtime","Which pattern and which runtime for this job?"],["Senses and craft","w13 to w15","Images and audio, no proof of quality, failures nobody can see","How do I know it works, and why did it break?"],["Operate and govern","w16 to w18","Cost, rate limits, outages, missing oversight, odd edge cases","How do I run it safely at scale?"],["Finale","w19","Mixed scenarios under time pressure","Can I defend a design out loud?"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"h","text":"Where does each failure belong","level":2,"id":"where-does-each-failure-belong"},{"t":"failure","title":"Symptom to world","items":[{"symptom":"The first token takes eight seconds","cause":"Long prefill, queueing, a cold prompt cache or a long generated answer.","fix":"Go to w02: measure time to first token, shape prompts for cache hits and stream."},{"symptom":"The assistant cannot cite our own content","cause":"The content was never ingested, was parsed badly or was split into useless chunks.","fix":"Go to w03 to w05: ingestion, chunking, embeddings and retrieval."},{"symptom":"One user sees another team's document","cause":"Permissions are missing from the index or a cache is shared across users.","fix":"Go to w07: security trimming, live permission checks and cache isolation."},{"symptom":"An agent loops between two tools","cause":"Vague tool descriptions or a pattern that does not match the task.","fix":"Go to w08 and w10: tool design and agent patterns."},{"symptom":"Quality dropped after a model update and nobody noticed","cause":"No evaluation set and no traces.","fix":"Go to w14 and w15: evals, tracing and alerts."},{"symptom":"The bill doubled in a month","cause":"Token growth, no routing, no caching and no limits.","fix":"Go to w01, w02 and w16: token economics, routing and cost controls."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Strong candidates say **where on the journey** a problem lives before they name a technology. A latency complaint and a leakage complaint need different questions, even when both mention the same assistant."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w00-l1-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each symptom to the world where you would look first.","pairs":[{"l":"Answers cite old policy because new files never reach the index","r":"w03 Data ingestion"},{"l":"Time to first token is eight seconds","r":"w02 Latency and caching"},{"l":"A user can retrieve another tenant's document","r":"w07 RAG security and ACLs"},{"l":"An agent loops between two tools","r":"w10 Agent patterns"}],"explain":"Each symptom has a home stop on the journey. Stale answers start at ingestion, slow first tokens belong to latency and caching, cross-tenant leaks belong to security trimming, and tool loops belong to agent design.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w00-l1-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the regions of the journey in the order a question meets them.","items":["Foundations: model, API, latency","Knowledge: ingest, chunk, retrieve, guard, secure","Agents and protocols: tools, MCP, A2A, agents","Senses and craft: multimodal inputs, evaluation, observability","Operate and govern: cost, reliability, oversight"],"explain":"The model and its limits come first, knowledge comes next, then tools and agents act on it. Proof of quality follows, and running the system safely at scale comes last.","difficulty":1}],"defend":null,"refs":["r23","r24"],"takeaways":["One question travels through twenty worlds, and every stop forces a choice","Name where on the journey a problem lives before you name a technology","Dials, not scenarios, decide which option wins"]},{"id":"w00-l2","order":2,"title":"How this guide plays","kind":"concept","minutes":15,"brief":"You have an hour with a group, or twenty minutes alone. Learn the loop each level follows, where your progress lives and which study path fits your time and your gaps.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The level loop","level":2,"id":"the-level-loop"},{"t":"flow","title":"Every problem level follows the same eight steps","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Brief","md":"A problem with its constraints left open on purpose.","tag":"read"},{"title":"Options board","md":"Every sensible option, with pros, cons, best-when and avoid-when.","tag":"compare"},{"title":"Flip the dials","md":"Change scale, permissions, latency, risk or cost and watch the ranking and the reasons move.","tag":"play"},{"title":"Failure modes","md":"Symptom, root cause and fix for what usually goes wrong.","tag":"study"},{"title":"Quiz","md":"Single choice, multi select, ordering, matching, numeric and bug-spotting items.","tag":"check"},{"title":"Defend it","md":"Write an interview-style answer, reveal the model answer, score yourself with the rubric.","tag":"speak"},{"title":"Flashcards","md":"Spaced-repetition cards that build up across worlds.","tag":"recall"},{"title":"References","md":"Dated links to specs, vendor docs, papers and courses.","tag":"verify"}]},{"t":"tabs","tabs":[{"label":"Saving and moving progress","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Progress lives in this browser's local storage on this computer. It is never sent anywhere, and the guide works with no network.","Clearing site data or switching browsers removes your progress. Export first from the footer or the Progress page.","To move to another browser or computer, export a file and import it there. Importing can merge with existing progress or replace it.","If the browser blocks local storage, a banner says so and the guide keeps progress in memory until you export."]}]},{"label":"Flashcards and keys","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Keys","What they do"],"rows":[["`/` or Ctrl+K or Cmd+K","Open quick search"],["`Esc`","Close search, a dialog or the navigation drawer"],["`[`","Show or hide the sidebar"],["`t`","Switch between light and dark theme"],["`?`","Open the help page at the shortcuts"],["`1` to `5`, then Enter","Quiz: choose an option, then check the answer"],["`Space`, then `1` to `4`","Flashcards: flip the card, then grade it"],["Left and right arrows","Tabs: move between variants"]],"compact":true}]},{"label":"Labs","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Seven interactive labs sit inside the worlds where the idea matters. World 2 holds `kv-cache`, `prompt-cache` and `latency-budget`. World 4 holds `chunk-visualizer`, world 5 holds `rrf-playground`, world 7 holds `acl-simulator` and world 10 holds `context-budget`. Each lab ends with three guided experiments."}]},{"label":"Boards at a glance","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Board","Use it to"],"rows":[["Compare","Read each option's pros, cons, best-when and avoid-when"],["Dials","Flip constraints and see the ranking and the reasons update"],["Scenario","Separate what you know from what you must ask"],["Failure","Match a symptom to a cause and a fix"],["Formula and stats","Check the numbers behind a decision"],["Accordion","Go deeper without cluttering the main path"]],"compact":true}]}]},{"t":"h","text":"Study strategy and facilitation","level":2,"id":"study-strategy-and-facilitation"},{"t":"table","caption":"Run one world as a workshop","head":["Step","What the facilitator does","Time"],"rows":[["Choose the scope","Pick one world per session. Most worlds take 60 to 90 minutes. Ask everyone to open the guide from the same zip so the content matches.","5 min"],["Turn on facilitator mode","It shows quiz answers and model answers with an Answer key badge. Keep it off on the learners' screens.","1 min"],["Run each level in four moves","Read the brief aloud. Let pairs flip the dials and argue. Take the quiz. Run the defend task with its timebox, then compare against the rubric.","3, 10 and 5 min, then the timebox"],["Debrief with the flip question","Ask what would change the choice. Good answers name a dial and a threshold.","5 min"],["Collect evidence","Ask each learner to export progress and share the file. The dashboard shows weak topics to coach next.","5 min"]]},{"t":"compare","title":"Three study paths","intro":"Pick by how much time you have and how much you already know.","options":[{"name":"Sequential tour","summary":"Work through w00 to w19 in order.","pros":["Every dial is introduced before it is used","Flashcards build up in order"],"cons":["Slow if you already know a region"],"bestWhen":"You are new to GenAI engineering and have several weeks.","avoidWhen":"An interview is days away."},{"name":"Gap-driven","summary":"Use the dashboard to find weak topics and revisit those levels.","pros":["Spends time where you are weakest"],"cons":["Needs some progress data first","Can skip prerequisite context"],"bestWhen":"You have finished a first pass and want to close gaps.","avoidWhen":"You have no quiz history yet."},{"name":"Interview sprint","summary":"Do the boss levels and defend tasks first, then read the levels behind each miss.","pros":["Practises speaking structure under a timebox","Shows gaps quickly"],"cons":["Boss levels assume the earlier worlds"],"bestWhen":"You know the basics and need fluency in days.","avoidWhen":"You cannot yet name the dials of a system."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Say it out loud","md":"The defend task rewards structure, and you build structure by speaking it. Answer aloud against the timebox before you reveal the model answer, then score yourself with the rubric."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w00-l2-q1","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every true statement about your progress in this guide.","options":["It is saved in browser local storage on this computer","Switching to another browser keeps it automatically","Importing a file can merge with or replace existing progress","Exported files are uploaded to a server for backup","If local storage is blocked, a banner appears and progress stays in memory until you export"],"answers":[0,2,4],"explain":"Progress is local to the browser, so switching browsers loses it unless you export and import. Nothing is ever uploaded, and a blocked local storage falls back to memory with a banner.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w00-l2-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A facilitator projects the guide on a screen the whole group can see. Which setting should be off, and why?","options":["Dark theme, because it hides the dials from people far from the screen","The sidebar, because it distracts the group from the quiz questions","Facilitator mode, because it shows the answers","Local storage, because it records every answer the group gives"],"answer":2,"explain":"Facilitator mode shows answers with an Answer key badge, so it spoils the quiz and the defend task on a shared screen. Theme and sidebar do not affect learning, and local storage keeps data private to the browser.","difficulty":1}],"defend":null,"refs":["r23"],"takeaways":["Every problem level runs the same loop: brief, options, dials, failures, quiz, defend, cards, references","Progress is local to the browser, so export before you clear data or switch machines","Facilitator mode reveals answers, so keep it off on learners' screens","Choose the study path by time and gaps, and always practise the defend task aloud"]},{"id":"w00-l3","order":3,"title":"Dial and defend in five moves","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"An interviewer asks how to make an assistant answer questions about information the model was never trained on. Notice what a strong answer asks before it names a technology, and practise the five moves on a gentle first board.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The five moves","level":2,"id":"the-five-moves"},{"t":"flow","title":"Dial-and-Defend","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Clarify the dials","md":"Ask about corpus, update rate, permission model, tenancy, latency, risk and cost.","tag":"1"},{"title":"List the options","md":"Name every sensible option, including the boring one.","tag":"2"},{"title":"Pick and flip","md":"Choose for the stated dials and say what would flip the choice.","tag":"3"},{"title":"Failure modes","md":"Say how each one would show up and how you would detect it.","tag":"4"},{"title":"Metrics and rollout","md":"State what you will measure and how you would roll it out safely.","tag":"5"}]},{"t":"scenario","title":"The worked example prompt","known":["The model has never seen the content","The interviewer gives no other detail"],"ask":["How big is the content, and how often does it change?","Do different users have different permissions?","Does the answer need citations or live data?","What is the latency budget, and what does a wrong answer cost?"],"tension":"You cannot choose until the dials are stated, so asking is the first move, not a delay."},{"t":"h","text":"Worked example: ground the assistant","level":2,"id":"worked-example-ground-the-assistant"},{"t":"compare","title":"Four ways to ground an assistant","intro":"World 1 compares them in depth. Here you only need the shape of each option.","options":[{"name":"Prompt only","summary":"Paste the knowledge into the prompt, ideally with prompt caching.","pros":["No infrastructure","The model reads the full text"],"cons":["Limited by the window and the cost of every call","No per-user filtering"],"bestWhen":"The content is small and stable, with one audience. Anthropic's rule of thumb is under about 200,000 tokens [[r26]].","avoidWhen":"The content is large, changes often or must be filtered per user."},{"name":"RAG","summary":"Retrieve the relevant chunks per question and ground the answer on them.","pros":["Scales to large corpora","Citations and per-user filters","Updates by re-indexing"],"cons":["Retrieval quality becomes the ceiling","More moving parts"],"bestWhen":"The corpus is large or changing, or permissions and citations matter.","avoidWhen":"The content is tiny and stable, so the machinery buys nothing."},{"name":"Tools","summary":"Give the model a lookup tool for live data, such as an API or a search endpoint.","pros":["Always current","Reads the system of record"],"cons":["Extra round trips","Tool design and permissions need care"],"bestWhen":"Data changes by the minute or lives behind an API.","avoidWhen":"You need reasoning over a whole document set with no live source."},{"name":"Fine-tuning","summary":"Train behaviour into the model weights.","pros":["Style, format and smaller models"],"cons":["Weak for facts. RAG beat unsupervised fine-tuning in a controlled study [[r27]]"],"bestWhen":"You need a tone, a format or a cheaper model.","avoidWhen":"You want the model to know your facts."}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w00-l3-ground","title":"How to ground an assistant","prompt":"Flip the corpus, the update rate and what you need. The ranking and the reasons update.","dials":[{"id":"size","label":"Corpus size","values":[{"id":"small","label":"Small, under about 200K tokens"},{"id":"large","label":"Large, millions of tokens"}]},{"id":"rate","label":"Update rate","values":[{"id":"rare","label":"Rarely changes"},{"id":"live","label":"Changes by the minute"}]},{"id":"need","label":"What you need","values":[{"id":"facts","label":"Cited facts from the content"},{"id":"style","label":"A tone or output format"}]}],"options":[{"id":"prompt","name":"Prompt only","summary":"Paste the content in."},{"id":"rag","name":"RAG","summary":"Retrieve per question."},{"id":"tools","name":"Tools","summary":"Look up live data."},{"id":"ft","name":"Fine-tuning","summary":"Train behaviour in."}],"scores":{"prompt":{"size":{"small":{"s":2,"why":"Under about 200,000 tokens the content can go straight into the prompt."},"large":{"s":-2,"why":"It will not fit, and long inputs lose accuracy."}},"rate":{"rare":{"s":1,"why":"A stable prompt is easy to maintain and cache."},"live":{"s":-1,"why":"Every change rewrites the prompt."}},"need":{"facts":{"s":1,"why":"The model reads the source text directly."},"style":{"s":1,"why":"Instructions and examples steer tone and format cheaply."}}},"rag":{"size":{"small":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but may be more machinery than needed."},"large":{"s":2,"why":"Retrieval scales past any window."}},"rate":{"rare":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"live":{"s":1,"why":"Re-index changed content without retraining."}},"need":{"facts":{"s":2,"why":"Retrieval gives grounded, citable answers."},"style":{"s":-1,"why":"Retrieval does not change tone or format."}}},"tools":{"size":{"small":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"large":{"s":1,"why":"A search tool can reach a corpus the prompt cannot hold."}},"rate":{"rare":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"live":{"s":2,"why":"A lookup tool reads the live source."}},"need":{"facts":{"s":1,"why":"Fresh facts, with citations if the tool returns sources."},"style":{"s":-2,"why":"A tool cannot change tone or format."}}},"ft":{"size":{"small":{"s":-1,"why":"Little to learn from, and the prompt can hold it."},"large":{"s":-1,"why":"Controlled studies favour RAG for facts at any size."}},"rate":{"rare":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"live":{"s":-2,"why":"Facts go stale and retraining is slow."}},"need":{"facts":{"s":-2,"why":"New facts are learned slowly and unreliably."},"style":{"s":2,"why":"Tone, format and smaller models are what tuning is for."}}}},"takeaway":"No option wins on every dial. Say which dial drove your pick, and which dial would flip it."},{"t":"table","caption":"A worked answer, move by move","head":["Move","What you say","Why it scores"],"rows":[["1. Clarify","\"How big is the content, how often does it change, who may see which part, how fast must it answer and what does a wrong answer cost?\"","Names the dials before any technology."],["2. Options","\"Prompt only, RAG, tools for live lookups and fine-tuning. The boring option is pasting the content into a cached prompt.\"","Includes the boring option as the baseline to beat."],["3. Pick and flip","\"For a small, stable corpus with one audience I would use the cached prompt. If the corpus outgrows the window or permissions become per user I move to RAG. If the data changes by the minute I add a lookup tool.\"","States dials and thresholds, not slogans."],["4. Failure modes","\"Stale answers, missing citations and truncated context. I would detect them with a golden question set and a groundedness check.\"","Pairs each failure with a detection signal."],["5. Metrics and rollout","\"Accuracy on the golden set, citation rate, p95 latency and cost per answer. I would run in shadow, then a small canary.\"","Names metrics and a safe rollout."]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Say the flip aloud","md":"The sentence that separates good answers from great ones is the flip: \"If the corpus outgrows the window, or permissions become per user, I switch to RAG.\" It names a dial and a threshold."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w00-l3-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the five Dial-and-Defend moves in order.","items":["Clarify the dials","List the options, including the boring one","Pick for the stated dials and say what would flip it","Name failure modes and how you would detect them","Name the metrics and the rollout plan"],"explain":"You cannot list options sensibly before you know the dials, and you cannot name failure modes before you have chosen. Metrics and rollout close the answer because they depend on the chosen design.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w00-l3-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"You chose a cached prompt for a small, stable corpus. The corpus then grows to millions of tokens and changes hourly. What does the board flip to?","options":["Fine-tune the model on the whole corpus again every hour","RAG with re-indexing as content changes","Keep the prompt and move to a bigger model with a larger window","Lower the temperature to keep the answers stable"],"answer":1,"explain":"Retrieval scales past the window and re-indexing handles hourly updates. Hourly fine-tuning is slow and weak for facts, a bigger model does not remove the window or price limits, and temperature does not change what the model knows.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r26"},{"id":"w00-l3-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A candidate answers: \"I would use a vector database and a large model at temperature 0.\" Which Dial-and-Defend move did they skip?","options":["Metrics and rollout","Naming failure modes","Listing a boring option","Clarifying the dials"],"answer":3,"explain":"They named a technology before asking about corpus size, update rate, permissions, latency, risk or cost. They also skipped other moves, but clarifying the dials is the one that makes every later choice defensible.","difficulty":1}],"defend":null,"refs":["r26","r27"],"takeaways":["Clarify the dials before you name a technology","Always offer the boring option as the baseline to beat","Pick for the stated dials and say which dial and threshold would flip the choice","Pair every failure mode with how you would detect it, then end on metrics and rollout"]},{"id":"w00-l4","order":4,"title":"Freshness map A: models, APIs and caching","kind":"concept","minutes":15,"brief":"Half of what you read about LLM APIs is out of date, and some of it breaks code. Use this dated map, checked on 2026-10-13, to test a fact before you say it in an answer.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"How to read this map","level":2,"id":"how-to-read-this-map"},{"t":"p","md":"Every row carries an absolute date and a source. Where a vendor gave no date, the row says so. Items that already happened are in the tabs. Items still ahead of 2026-10-13 are in the last table, so you can pin and test replacements before they land."},{"t":"tabs","tabs":[{"label":"Models and APIs","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Date","Change","Why it matters"],"rows":[["2025-03-11","OpenAI releases the **Responses API** with built-in tools [[r1]].","Many tutorials still teach Chat Completions or Assistants."],["2025-08-07","The GPT-5 family reaches the API [[r1]].","Reasoning models changed which parameters are accepted."],["2025-08","Azure OpenAI offers an opt-in **v1 API** [[r28]].","Base URL ends `/openai/v1` and a dated `api-version` is no longer required."],["2025-08-20","OpenAI releases the **Conversations API** [[r1]].","A durable conversation object exists, and it is not ZDR eligible [[r29]]."],["2025-08-26","OpenAI announces the **Assistants API** deprecation, with sunset on 2026-08-26 [[r4]].","It shut down on 2026-08-26. Threads map to Conversations and Runs to Responses."],["2025-12-11 and 2026-02-10","OpenAI adds client-side `/responses/compact`, then server-side compaction through `context_management` [[r1]].","Context management is an API feature, not only your code."],["2026-03-05","GPT-5.4: Chat Completions rejects tools when reasoning effort is not `none` [[r3]].","Old agent code breaks on upgrade. Use Responses."],["2026-04-16","Claude Opus 4.7: a new tokenizer (about 30% more tokens), `xhigh` effort, and non-default sampling values return 400 [[r7]].","Token budgets and temperature code both break."],["2026-09-03","GPT-6 Astra: async tool calling, and `reasoning.effort: none` returns 400 [[r30]].","Port effort settings, do not copy them."],["2026-09-22 to 2026-10-07","Claude Opus 5.5 (thinking cannot be disabled), Sonnet 5.5 (forced `tool_choice` returns 400) and Haiku 5.5 [[r7]].","Code written for older Claude models can break."],["2026-09-29","GPT-6.1 Sol: Chat Completions works only without tools [[r30]].","Tools need Responses."]]}]},{"label":"Caching and cost","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Date","Change","Why it matters"],"rows":[["2026-07-09 (release of the GPT-5.6 family; the exact change date for caching is unverified)","The GPT-5.6 family adds explicit prompt caching controls [[r1]]. Cache writes bill at 1.25x the input rate, `prompt_cache_options` and explicit breakpoints appear, 128-token rounding is gone, `prompt_cache_retention` is deprecated and `prompt_cache_key` becomes optional [[r5]].","Older models return 400 for the new fields."],["2026-09-03 and later","Moving from GPT-5.5 or earlier to GPT-6 replaces `prompt_cache_retention` with `prompt_cache_options.ttl` (`\"30m\"`) [[r30]].","The cost model changes, so recompute savings."],["2026-09-08","Prompt Cache Diagnostics is GA in Responses for GPT-5.6 and later [[r1]].","Use it to debug cache misses."],["2026-04-24 (version date of `gpt-5.5`)","Azure enables extended retention by default on `gpt-5.5` but `in_memory` on `gpt-5.4` and older [[r6]].","The same code caches differently by model."],["2026-07-28","The MCP revision adds cache hints (`ttlMs`, `cacheScope`) and makes deterministic tool order a SHOULD [[r8]].","A reshuffled tool list also breaks provider prompt caches."],["2026-09-25","Azure Flex: `service_tier: \"flex\"` on a model without Flex support returns 400 instead of falling back to Standard [[r31]].","Do not assume a graceful fallback."]]}]},{"label":"Wind-downs","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Date","Change","Why it matters"],"rows":[["2026-05-07","OpenAI starts winding down self-serve fine-tuning [[r2]].","Plan alternatives. Active customers lose job creation on 2027-01-06."],["2026-06-03","OpenAI deprecates the Evals platform (read-only 2026-10-31, shutdown 2026-11-30), reusable prompts (`v1/prompts`, shutdown 2026-11-30) and Agent Builder (shutdown 2026-11-30) [[r2]].","The Assistants migration table still maps Assistants to Prompts."],["2026-06-11","OpenAI announces shutdown of the `gpt-5-2025-08-07`, `gpt-5-mini`, `gpt-5-nano`, `gpt-5-pro`, `o3` and `o3-pro` snapshots for 2026-12-11 [[r2]].","Azure keeps `gpt-5` until 2027-02-09 [[r18]], so dates depend on the platform."],["2026-10-01","OpenAI deprecates `gpt-5.3-codex`, `gpt-5.1` and `gpt-5.4-nano`, with shutdown on 2027-04-01 [[r2]].","Six months of notice."]]}]}]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w00-d-api-timeline","title":"Seven dates that changed API code","alt":"A timeline from the Responses API release in 2025 through the Assistants deprecation, the GPT-5.4 tool rule, the GPT-5.6 caching model, the Assistants sunset, GPT-6 Astra and the first large shutdowns.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[2025-03-11 Responses API] --> B[2025-08-26 Assistants deprecated]\n  B --> C[2026-03-05 GPT-5.4 tools need Responses]\n  C --> D[2026-07-09 GPT-5.6 caching model]\n  D --> E[2026-08-26 Assistants sunset]\n  E --> F[2026-09-03 GPT-6 Astra]\n  F --> G[2026-10-23 Large shutdowns]","caption":"The last node is still ahead of 2026-10-13."},{"t":"h","text":"Upcoming model and API dates to pin","level":2,"id":"upcoming-model-and-api-dates-to-pin"},{"t":"table","caption":"Dates after 2026-10-13","head":["Date","What happens","Platform"],"rows":[["2026-10-23","Shutdown of `gpt-4`, `gpt-4-turbo`, `gpt-3.5-turbo`, `o1`, `o3-mini`, `o4-mini`, `gpt-4.1-nano`, `gpt-4o-2024-05-13` and related fine-tunes [[r2]].","OpenAI API"],["2026-10-31","The Evals platform becomes read-only [[r2]].","OpenAI API"],["2026-11-19","Azure retires `o1`, `o1-pro`, `o3`, `o3-mini`, `o3-pro`, `o4-mini` and `o3-deep-research` [[r18]].","Azure OpenAI"],["2026-11-30","Shutdown of Evals, reusable prompts and Agent Builder [[r2]].","OpenAI API"],["2026-12-09","Azure retires `gpt-4o` 2024-05-13 [[r18]].","Azure OpenAI"],["2026-12-11","Shutdown of the `gpt-5` snapshots named above [[r2]].","OpenAI API"],["2027-01-06","Active customers can no longer create fine-tuning jobs [[r2]].","OpenAI API"],["2027-04-14","Azure retires the `gpt-4.1` family [[r18]].","Azure OpenAI"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"How to read the labels","md":"Statuses are what the source said on 2026-10-13: GA, Preview, Beta, Deprecated or unverified. Flex processing is Beta. A date marked unverified means I could not find the vendor's own date. Check the source before you quote it. What flips a recommendation: a Preview label, a missing SLA or a retirement date inside your delivery window."},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: keep your own map fresh","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ol","items":["Subscribe to three feeds: the OpenAI changelog and deprecations pages, the Claude Platform release notes, and the Microsoft Learn what's new and retirement schedule pages.","When you cite a limit, a default or an availability, write the date you checked it and the status.","Pin model versions in production, keep an eval set, and test the replacement as soon as a retirement notice appears.","Expect platform differences: the same model can retire on a different date on Azure than on the OpenAI API."]}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w00-l4-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put these events in date order, earliest first.","items":["OpenAI releases the Responses API","OpenAI announces the Assistants API deprecation","GPT-5.4 stops Chat Completions from using tools with reasoning on","The Assistants API shuts down","GPT-6 Astra is released"],"explain":"The dates are 2025-03-11, 2025-08-26, 2026-03-05, 2026-08-26 and 2026-09-03. Knowing the order helps you spot tutorials that predate each change.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w00-l4-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"An agent written in 2025 uses Chat Completions with function tools on a reasoning model, and it fails after the team upgrades to GPT-5.4. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Function tools were removed from every OpenAI API in the upgrade","The Assistants API shutdown also blocks Chat Completions calls","Reasoning models cannot call functions in any OpenAI API","Chat Completions rejects tools with reasoning on"],"answer":3,"explain":"From GPT-5.4 on, Chat Completions does not support tool calling with reasoning effort other than none, so the agent must move to Responses or run with effort none. Tools still exist in Responses, the Assistants shutdown is unrelated, and reasoning models can call functions.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r3"},{"id":"w00-l4-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"OpenAI's Evals platform becomes read-only on 2026-10-31. How many days of write access remain after 2026-10-13?","answer":18,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"days","hint":"Count from 2026-10-13 to 2026-10-31 in the same month.","explain":"31 minus 13 is 18 days. The platform then stays read-only until the shutdown on 2026-11-30, so export what you need before the write window closes.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r2"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r5","r6","r7","r8","r18","r28","r29","r30","r31"],"takeaways":["Date and label every product fact, and say where a date is unverified","Responses, Conversations, compaction and GPT-5.4 changed what API code looks like","Caching rules changed with GPT-5.6 and again for GPT-6, so recompute savings","Pin the shutdown dates ahead of you, because Azure and OpenAI retire the same model on different days"]},{"id":"w00-l5","order":5,"title":"Freshness map B: protocols, platforms and policy","kind":"concept","minutes":15,"brief":"Protocol revisions, agent platforms and regulations all moved in 2025 and 2026, and some pages still teach the old shape. Use this map, checked on 2026-10-13, to label what is GA, what is preview and what has a date on it.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Protocols, platforms and policy","level":2,"id":"protocols-platforms-and-policy"},{"t":"tabs","tabs":[{"label":"MCP and A2A","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Date","Change","Why it matters"],"rows":[["2025-03-26","MCP: Streamable HTTP replaces HTTP+SSE, which is formally deprecated [[r8]].","Tutorials with `/sse` and `/messages` endpoints are legacy."],["2025-06-18","MCP servers become OAuth resource servers, and clients must send the RFC 8707 `resource` parameter [[r8]].","A server that doubles as its own authorization server no longer matches the spec."],["2025-11-25","MCP: client ID metadata documents (CIMD) recommended, URL elicitation added, Tasks introduced as experimental [[r8]].","Dynamic client registration stops being the default advice."],["2025-12-09","MCP moves under the Agentic AI Foundation [[r9]].","Governance is Linux Foundation hosted. The maintainer and proposal process stayed the same."],["2026-03-12","A2A v1.0.0 [[r10]].","Breaking renames and enum casing. Clients on 0.3 still exist, so version negotiation matters."],["2026-05-28","A2A v1.0.1 [[r32]].","A patch: HTTP+JSON SHOULD use `application/a2a+json`. The spec page header can still show 1.0.0."],["2026-07-28","MCP revision: stateless protocol (no `initialize`, no session id, `server/discover` required), multi round-trip requests replace server push, sampling, roots, logging and dynamic client registration are deprecated, Tasks move to an extension, resumable SSE is removed [[r8]].","Several breaking changes. State must be an explicit handle that you authorize."],["2026-10-13","Foundry serves A2A preview 0.3 when a request has no version header and no `a2a-version` query value. A header and a query value must agree or you get 400 [[r11]].","Set the A2A version explicitly."]]}]},{"label":"Agent platforms","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Date","Change","Why it matters"],"rows":[["2026-04-03","Microsoft Agent Framework 1.0 for .NET and Python [[r12]].","Stable at GA: core agents, middleware, graph workflows with checkpointing, orchestrations (sequential, concurrent, handoff, group chat, Magentic-One), declarative YAML, MCP and migration assistants. Preview at GA: DevUI, Foundry hosted agent integration, Foundry tools, memory, observability and evaluations, AG-UI, Skills and the GitHub Copilot SDK and Claude Code SDK agents. A2A 1.0 support was still \"coming soon\" in the announcement."],["2026-05-01","Microsoft Agent 365 is GA for the Commercial segment, per user [[r15]].","At least one user needs a qualifying Agent 365 license, and Microsoft E5 is the best-fit prerequisite. It observes, governs and secures agents through a registry, Entra, Purview and Defender."],["2026-06-25","Foundry projects created after this date can use a private Azure Container Registry for hosted agents [[r25]].","Check the project's creation date before you promise private networking."],["2026-08","New Foundry articles cover long-running hosted agents: resilience, crash recovery, steering an in-flight turn, reconnecting streams and a human approval step. All are marked preview [[r14]].","Background mode and resilient execution solve different problems."],["2026-08-20","Support for the initial-preview hosted agent backend ends [[r33]].","Redeploy on the new model. Existing deployments are not migrated automatically."],["2026-12-01","Foundry Workflows (preview) retire [[r25]].","Use Microsoft Agent Framework for new development."]]}]},{"label":"Knowledge, safety and policy","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Date","Change","Why it matters"],"rows":[["2025-11","Content Understanding is GA with API `2025-11-01`. `2026-06-01-preview` adds signature detection and metadata extraction [[r20]].","Use the GA version for production."],["2026-04","Azure AI Search `2026-04-01` is the stable data-plane version. Index projections, the Content Understanding skill and the GenAI Prompt skill are GA [[r16]] [[r17]].","Older tutorials still show preview syntax."],["2026-04","Knowledge sources are GA on `2026-04-01`, but `ingestionPermissionOptions`, which carries ACLs, stays preview. The latest preview is `2026-08-01-preview` [[r16]].","A GA label on a source does not mean GA access control."],["2026-10-14","Azure OpenAI On Your Data retires, one day after the check date [[r19]].","Migrate to Foundry Agent Service with Foundry IQ."],["2025-12-09","OWASP publishes the Top 10 for Agentic Applications 2026, with ids ASI01 to ASI10 [[r21]].","A shared vocabulary for agent threats."],["2026-07-24 and 2026-07-27","The EU AI Omnibus, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, is published and in force. The dates come from a secondary source [[r22]].","Annex III high-risk rules start 2027-12-02 and Annex I rules start 2028-08-02. Posts that say 2026-08-02 for high-risk are out of date."]]}]}]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w00-d-mrtr","title":"Multi round-trip requests replace server push","alt":"The client calls a tool. The server answers with input_required. The client retries with the user's input and the server returns the result.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant C as Client\n  participant S as Server\n  C->>S: tools/call\n  S-->>C: input_required\n  C->>S: Retry with the user input\n  S-->>C: Result","caption":"MCP revision 2026-07-28. A server can no longer push an elicitation request on a stream [[r8]]."},{"t":"h","text":"What was GA and what was Preview on 2026-10-13","level":2,"id":"what-was-ga-and-what-was-preview-on-2026-10-13"},{"t":"table","caption":"Foundry capability status from the general-availability overview [[r25]]","head":["Capability","Status on 2026-10-13","Say this in an answer"],"rows":[["Agents (core)","GA","The core agent service is GA. Check hosted agents separately."],["Hosted agents as a separate item","No separate row. The concept page has no preview banner","I found no explicit GA statement, so say \"verify the status\" [[r13]]."],["Tracing","GA for prompt and hosted agents, Preview for workflow and external agents","Name the agent type."],["Toolboxes, publishing to Copilot and Teams","GA","Safe to rely on, within the docs' limits."],["Memory, agent guardrails, controls and intervention, monitoring, AI Gateway","Preview","No SLA. Plan for change."],["Voice Live in agents","Preview","Label it preview."],["Agent optimizer","Limited preview","Do not plan around it."],["Foundry IQ","Partial GA: API-level GA, portal access Preview","Say which surface you mean."],["Workflows","Preview, retiring 2026-12-01","Do not start new work on it."]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"A GA label can be narrower than it looks","md":"Agents (core) is GA, but hosted agents have no separate GA row. Knowledge sources are GA, but the permission option that carries ACLs is preview. Evaluations are GA, but some evaluators are preview. In an answer, name the exact surface and its status. A preview label, a missing SLA or a retirement date inside your delivery window should flip you toward a different option or a fallback."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w00-l5-q1","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every true statement about the MCP revision dated 2026-07-28.","options":["There is no `initialize` call and no session id","Servers push `elicitation/create` on an open stream","Tasks moved to an extension","Sampling, roots, logging and dynamic client registration are deprecated","JSON-RPC batching was added"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"The revision is stateless, moves Tasks to an extension and deprecates sampling, roots, logging and dynamic client registration. Server push for elicitation was replaced by input_required retries, and JSON-RPC batching was added in 2025-03-26 and removed in 2025-06-18.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r8"},{"id":"w00-l5-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A client sends A2A requests to a Foundry agent endpoint with no version header and no `a2a-version` query value, and the response shape matches an older protocol version. What explains this?","options":["Foundry always serves A2A v1.0.1 to every client that connects","Foundry defaults to preview 0.3 without a version","The request is missing an OAuth scope for the agent","A2A has no versions, only MCP has versioned releases"],"answer":1,"explain":"With no header and no query value, Foundry serves preview version 0.3, and a header and query value that disagree return 400. A missing OAuth scope would fail authorization, and A2A has versioned releases such as v1.0.0 and v1.0.1.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r11"},{"id":"w00-l5-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each date to the event.","pairs":[{"l":"2026-03-12","r":"A2A v1.0.0 is released"},{"l":"2026-04-03","r":"Agent Framework 1.0 is announced"},{"l":"2026-05-01","r":"Agent 365 is GA for the Commercial segment"},{"l":"2026-08-20","r":"Initial-preview hosted agent backend support ends"}],"explain":"These four dates are the anchors of the platform story: the protocol reached 1.0, the framework reached 1.0, the control plane reached GA and the first hosted agent backend was retired.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r12"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r8","r9","r10","r11","r12","r13","r14","r15","r16","r17","r19","r20","r21","r22","r25","r32","r33"],"takeaways":["MCP and A2A both changed shape in 2026, so check the spec revision and the version header","Agent Framework 1.0 and Agent 365 are GA, but several neighbouring features were preview on 2026-10-13","A GA label can be narrower than it looks, so name the exact surface and status","Policy dates move too: the EU AI Omnibus pushed high-risk rules to 2027-12-02 and 2028-08-02"]},{"id":"w00-l6","order":6,"title":"Boss warm-up: draft the replies","kind":"boss","minutes":12,"brief":"A team wants an assistant that drafts replies to customer emails. They have not said how many, how risky or how fast. Practise all five moves in three minutes, aloud.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Warm up before the real boss levels","level":2,"id":"warm-up-before-the-real-boss-levels"},{"t":"flow","title":"Your 3-minute shape","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Clarify","md":"Three or four questions about volume, risk, data needs and review.","tag":"30 s"},{"title":"Options","md":"Name three or four, including the boring one.","tag":"30 s"},{"title":"Pick and flip","md":"Choose for assumed dials and say what flips it.","tag":"60 s"},{"title":"Failure modes","md":"Two failures, each with a detection signal.","tag":"30 s"},{"title":"Metrics and rollout","md":"Two metrics and a staged rollout.","tag":"30 s"}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"How to use this level","md":"Set a timer for three minutes and answer aloud before you open the model answer. Then score yourself with the rubric. The goal is the shape, not a perfect design."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w00-l6-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which opening is strongest for this question?","options":["I would fine-tune a model on our past replies so it learns the company voice and policy","I would build RAG with a vector database over every past email in the archive","I would first ask about volume, tone rules, approval and the cost of a wrong reply","I would use the largest available model at temperature 0 and send the drafts directly"],"answer":2,"explain":"The strongest answers ask for dials before naming a technology. Fine-tuning is weak for facts, a vector database may be unnecessary, and the largest model at temperature 0 sends outbound text with no review or risk analysis.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w00-l6-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every item that belongs in step 5, metrics and rollout.","options":["Share of drafts that humans send with no edits","Policy violations caught per thousand drafts","A staged rollout: shadow mode, then a small canary","The list of options you considered","The dials you asked about at the start"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Edit rate and violation rate are metrics, and a staged rollout is the rollout plan. The options belong in step 2 and the dials belong in step 1.","difficulty":1},{"id":"w00-l6-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"You proposed human-reviewed drafts. The team asks to auto-send replies for one low-risk category. Which dial moved, and what changes?","options":["Risk fell for one category, so auto-send there with monitoring","Risk is irrelevant to the design, so remove review for every category","Volume moved, so switch to fine-tuning for every reply category","Latency moved, so remove evaluation because the reviewers are gone"],"answer":0,"explain":"The risk dial moved for one category, so the flip is narrow: auto-send there with monitoring and a rollback switch, and keep review for the rest. Removing review everywhere ignores risk, and the other options change dials that did not move.","difficulty":2}],"defend":{"prompt":"A team wants an assistant that drafts replies to customer emails. How would you approach it?","timebox":"3 min","clarify":["How many emails arrive per day, and how fast must a draft appear?","Who reviews drafts before sending, and what does a wrong reply cost?","What data do replies need: policy documents, past replies or live account data?","Which tone and compliance rules apply, and is any customer data restricted?","Which languages and channels are in scope?"],"options":[{"name":"Prompt with templates and style rules","when":"A small set of reply types and stable policies.","tradeoff":"Cheap and simple, but policies drift and long prompts raise cost per draft."},{"name":"RAG over policies and past replies","when":"A large or changing policy set where citations help reviewers.","tradeoff":"Retrieval quality becomes the ceiling, and it adds moving parts."},{"name":"Tools for account and order lookups","when":"Replies need live data from a system of record.","tradeoff":"Tool permissions and extra latency need care."},{"name":"Fine-tuning for tone","when":"A consistent voice is needed at high volume, or a smaller model is wanted.","tradeoff":"Weak for facts, so it does not replace retrieval."},{"name":"Human approval before sending","when":"A wrong reply is costly.","tradeoff":"Slower and needs reviewer time."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify the dials.** I would ask how many emails arrive and how fast a draft must appear, who reviews drafts and what a wrong reply costs, what data a reply needs, and which tone and compliance rules apply."},{"t":"p","md":"**2. List the options, including the boring one.** The boring option is a prompt with templates and style rules. The alternatives are RAG over policies and past replies, tools for live account lookups, fine-tuning for tone and human approval before sending."},{"t":"p","md":"**3. Pick for the stated dials, and say what flips it.** With no more detail I would assume moderate volume, a policy set that changes monthly, per-customer data and a human reviewer. I would start with a prompt plus RAG over the policies, a lookup tool for order data and human approval. If one reply category is low risk I would allow auto-send there. If the policy set is tiny and stable I would drop RAG and use a cached prompt. If volume grows a lot I would add routing and caching."},{"t":"p","md":"**4. Name failure modes and how I would detect them.** A draft invents a policy, which I would catch with a groundedness check and a golden set of real emails. A draft leaks another customer's data, which I would catch with access checks on every lookup and a leakage test set."},{"t":"p","md":"**5. Name metrics and the rollout.** I would track the share of drafts sent with no edits, policy violations per thousand drafts, p95 time to a draft and cost per draft. I would run in shadow mode first, then a small canary, with a switch to roll back."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about volume, risk, data needs and review before naming a technology","w":3},{"pt":"Names at least three options, including the boring one","w":2},{"pt":"Picks for stated or assumed dials instead of a favourite tool","w":2},{"pt":"States at least one explicit flip condition with a dial and a threshold","w":3},{"pt":"Names failure modes with a detection signal","w":2},{"pt":"Names metrics and a staged rollout","w":2}],"followUps":[{"q":"The team wants to auto-send password-reset replies. What changes?","a":"The risk dial is low for that category, so allow auto-send only there with groundedness and policy checks and a rollback switch. Keep review elsewhere and watch the edit rate and complaint rate."},{"q":"Volume grows tenfold. What breaks first?","a":"Latency and cost per draft, and retrieval load. Flip toward routing, prompt caching and batch processing for non-urgent replies, which worlds 1, 2 and 16 cover."},{"q":"How would you test the assistant before launch?","a":"Build a golden set of real emails with approved replies, judge drafts with a rubric plus human spot checks, and compare in shadow mode before any customer sees a draft. World 14 covers evaluation."}],"redFlags":["Names a vector database or a model before asking any question","Treats fine-tuning as the way to add policy knowledge","Offers one option only","Ignores risk and review for outbound customer messages","Ends without metrics or a rollout plan"]},"refs":["r23","r24","r26","r27"],"takeaways":["Practise the five moves aloud against a timebox until the shape is automatic","Assume dials when you cannot ask, and say that you are assuming","A good flip names one dial and one threshold","Close with metrics and a staged rollout, not with a tool name"]}],"topics":[{"id":"w00-t01","order":1,"group":"Start here","title":"How this guide works","minutes":6,"difficulty":"core","summary":"The guide is a set of standalone topic pages you can open in any order, plus an optional journey of practice problems, labs, flashcards and a progress file you can export.","useIf":"You want to revise one subject fast, follow the whole journey, or run a group session.","avoidIf":"You expect a linear course. Topic pages assume you can jump in and use the related links for depth.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"This guide teaches AI engineering through **options and trade-offs**, never through one fixed scenario. The main unit is the **topic**: one page you can open from the left menu and revise in about seven minutes. Topics sit inside **chapters** (for example w01 LLM controls and APIs). The sidebar switch **Topics | Journey** chooses between jumping to any topic (the default) and following one question through the stack in order."},{"t":"flow","title":"Anatomy of a topic page","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"What it is","md":"The mechanism in 150 to 400 words, with a visual."},{"title":"Where and when","md":"Concrete places, then use, avoid and flip conditions stated as dials."},{"title":"Alternatives","md":"A table with a loses-when column."},{"title":"Pitfalls","md":"Symptom, cause and fix."},{"title":"Scenarios","md":"Trade-off cards: choose, then see every verdict."},{"title":"Quick checks","md":"Quiz items and flashcards."}]},{"t":"p","md":"**Practice problems** and **boss rounds** (the Journey mode) turn the same material into game-style levels with dial boards and an interview-style defend task. **Labs** are small interactive tools, seven in total (for example the KV cache sizer and the RRF fusion playground). **Flashcards** use five Leitner boxes and four grades: again, hard, good and easy."},{"t":"p","md":"Progress lives in your browser's `localStorage` under the key `ftq.v1`. Export it from the Progress page as `ftq-progress-<name>-<date>.json` and import it later with **merge** (keeps the best of both files, the default) or **replace**. If the browser blocks storage, the guide shows a banner that asks you to export. **Facilitator mode** shows quiz and model answers with an Answer key badge, so keep it off on learners' screens. Press `/` or Ctrl or Cmd K to search."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Print a chapter cheat sheet","md":"Every chapter has a generated cheat sheet at `#/w/<chapter id>/cheatsheet`. It lists each topic's title, summary, use-if and avoid-if lines, which makes it a fast pre-interview scan."}],"whereToUse":["Before an interview: open the chapter cheat sheet, then drill the topics where your answers feel vague","On a commute or between meetings: one topic, its scenario cards and its flashcards","In a team session: one chapter per session, with the facilitator mode on the host's screen only","After a platform change: re-open the topic and check its dated references, then read the freshness topic","When onboarding a colleague: send the topic links in the order of your stack"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You know the subject and need to sharpen trade-off reasoning","You have a gap in one chapter and little time","You want evidence of progress that you can share as a file","You coach others and need a structure for a debrief"],"avoid":["You have never heard of the area and need a tutorial first: start with the introductory course in the references, then return","You need a vendor's current price or limit: the guide dates its facts, so confirm in the live documentation","You want to memorise answers: scenario cards change verdicts when a dial moves, so rote answers fail"],"flips":["Time is under 30 minutes: use the cheat sheet and flashcards, skip the practice problems","You study in a group: switch to Journey mode so everyone meets the same boss round","Your browser clears storage: export progress after every session"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to use the guide","head":["Mode","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Topic-first revision (default)","You know which subject is weak and want depth fast","You do not know your gaps yet","Can leave whole chapters unread"],["Journey of practice problems","You want to rehearse a full end-to-end answer","You have little time","Longer, and levels repeat topic ideas"],["Flashcards only","You already understand and need recall","You have never met the concept","Cards without context teach slogans"],["Facilitated group session","A team needs shared vocabulary and debate","Learners study alone","Needs a host who keeps answers hidden"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Progress vanishes after a browser clean-up","cause":"State is stored in `localStorage`, which the browser can clear","fix":"Export the JSON file after each session and import it with merge on the new browser"},{"symptom":"Learners see answers during a group session","cause":"Facilitator mode is a per-browser setting and was left on","fix":"Turn it on only on the host's browser and check the Answer key badge before sharing a screen"},{"symptom":"A learner reads topics in order and feels lost","cause":"Topics are standalone and assume a short refresher, not a lesson sequence","fix":"Use the related links at the bottom of each topic, or switch to Journey mode for the story"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w00-t01-s1","title":"Two weeks to an interview","situation":"A candidate has two weeks. They use the platform daily at work but have never explained their design choices aloud.","constraints":["Study time is 30 to 45 minutes a day","The interview is a design conversation, not a coding test","The candidate builds well but explains poorly"],"unknowns":["Which chapters match the role?","Is a mock interview partner available?"],"question":"How should the candidate use the guide?","options":[{"name":"Read every topic once in chapter order","verdict":"good","why":"Complete coverage and a fixed plan.","tradeoff":"At 7 minutes a topic, 22 topics in one chapter already take more than two hours, so coverage thins out."},{"name":"Topic-first on weak chapters, scenario cards daily, boss rounds spoken aloud","verdict":"best","why":"Scenario cards train the trade-off language and boss rounds train the spoken structure, which is the stated weakness.","tradeoff":"Skipping other chapters leaves blind spots outside the role focus."},{"name":"Flashcards only","verdict":"poor","why":"Recall of parameter names does not train justification, which is where the candidate is weak.","tradeoff":"Flashcards are right for the last two days, once the reasoning is in place."}],"flips":[{"if":"The candidate also lacks basic vocabulary","then":"Start with the introductory course in the references, then follow the same plan."},{"if":"Only three days remain","then":"Use the cheat sheets for the target chapters and do one boss round aloud."}],"takeaway":"Match the mode to the weakness: reasoning needs scenarios, recall needs cards."},{"id":"w00-t01-s2","title":"Team workshop on a shared laptop","situation":"A lead runs a 90 minute session for eight engineers. One laptop is projected. Learners also have the guide open on their own machines.","constraints":["One chapter per session","Answers must stay hidden until groups commit","Learners want a record afterwards"],"unknowns":["Do learners use the same browser profile each time?","Is there a printer for cheat sheets?"],"question":"How do you set it up?","options":[{"name":"Turn facilitator mode on for everyone to speed up review","verdict":"poor","why":"Learners see verdicts before they commit, so the trade-off debate never happens.","tradeoff":"Right for a self-study learner who wants to check an answer after attempting it."},{"name":"Let learners read the chapter alone, then discuss","verdict":"good","why":"Quiet reading suits deep material.","tradeoff":"Loses the live disagreement that exposes unstated assumptions."},{"name":"Facilitator mode on the host laptop only, pairs argue each card, everyone exports progress at the end","verdict":"best","why":"Answers show only where the host controls them, and exported files give each learner a record.","tradeoff":"The host spends time on the debrief and the export step."}],"flips":[{"if":"Learners are remote","then":"Share the host screen with facilitator mode on and have learners vote before each reveal."},{"if":"The group is new to the topic","then":"Run Journey mode so they meet the story first."}],"takeaway":"Keep answers on the host screen and make learners commit before the reveal."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w00-t01-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Where does the guide keep your progress by default?","options":["On a server tied to your learner name","In a cookie that expires after 24 hours","In the browser's `localStorage`, exportable as a JSON file","Inside the zip file you opened"],"answer":2,"explain":"State sits in `localStorage` under `ftq.v1` and can be exported and imported. There is no server, cookies are not used for progress, and the zip is read only."},{"id":"w00-t01-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which statements about importing a progress file are true?","options":["Merge keeps the best of both records","Replace overwrites the current state with the file","Import needs a network connection","Merge is the default mode"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Merge keeps the better record per key and is the default, and replace takes the file as is. Import is a local operation and needs no network."},{"id":"w00-t01-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the parts of a topic page in the order you meet them.","items":["What it is","Where and when to use it","Alternatives table","Pitfalls","Scenario cards","Quick checks and flashcards"],"explain":"The page moves from mechanism to decision criteria to comparison to failure, then tests you with scenarios and quick checks."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the main unit of this guide?","back":"The topic: a standalone page that explains, says where and when to use it, compares alternatives, lists pitfalls and offers scenario cards."},{"front":"How do you keep progress across browsers?","back":"Export the progress JSON from the Progress page and import it elsewhere, using merge to keep the best of both files."},{"front":"What does facilitator mode do?","back":"It reveals quiz and model answers with an Answer key badge. Enable it only on the host's screen."}],"related":["w00-t02","w00-t03","w00-t04","w00-l1","w00-l2"],"refs":["r23","r24"]},{"id":"w00-t02","order":2,"group":"Start here","title":"The GenAI system map","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A GenAI product is a stack of decisions, from the model call to knowledge, tools, agents, proof and operations. Each chapter owns some of those decisions.","useIf":"You need to place a problem on the stack before you pick a technology.","avoidIf":"You already know which layer is failing and need the details of that layer.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A user question travels through layers. First the **model call** fixes the token budget, the controls and the API style (w01), and the **latency and caching** layer decides what is affordable (w02). **Knowledge** layers get the right content to the model: ingestion, chunking and indexes, retrieval, guardrails and access control (w03 to w07). **Tools and protocols** let the model act and talk to other systems (w08 MCP, w09 A2A). **Agents** coordinate several steps (w10 patterns, w11 Microsoft Agent Framework, w12 Foundry hosted agents and Agent 365)."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w00-t02-d1","title":"One question, nine stops","alt":"A question passes through model and API, latency and cache, knowledge, tools and protocols and agents to an answer, which is then checked by senses, proof and operations chapters.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Question] --> B[Budget and API w01]\n  B --> L[Latency and cache w02]\n  L --> K[Knowledge w03 to w07]\n  K --> T[Tools and protocols w08 w09]\n  T --> A[Agents w10 to w12]\n  A --> R[Answer]\n  R --> S[Senses w13]\n  R --> P[Proof w14 w15]\n  R --> O[Operate w16 w17]"},{"t":"p","md":"After the answer exists, three more concerns apply to every layer: **senses** (images and audio, w13), **proof** (evaluation and observability, w14 and w15) and **operations** (cost, reliability, governance, w16 and w17). Field notes (w18) and boss rounds (w19) combine them."},{"t":"table","caption":"Which chapter owns the decision","head":["Symptom","Likely layer","Chapter"],"rows":[["Parameter rejected, output cut off, wrong API","Model call","w01"],["First token is slow, bill too high from repeated prompts","Latency and caching","w02"],["Answer is missing from the system, or chunks are cut badly","Ingestion, chunking","w03, w04"],["Right chunk exists but is not retrieved","Retrieval","w05"],["Model answers anyway, or leaks another user's data","Guardrails, access control","w06, w07"],["Tool misuse, protocol mismatch","Tools and interop","w08, w09"],["Loops, wrong runtime, unclear ownership","Agents","w10 to w12"],["Nobody can show it works or why it broke","Proof","w14, w15"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"why","title":"Start with the least machinery","md":"Anthropic's guide to building agents advises finding the simplest solution possible, because agentic systems trade latency and cost for task performance [[tr1]]. Climb the stack only when an eval shows the lower layer fails."}],"whereToUse":["Triage: a complaint arrives and you must decide who owns the investigation","Design reviews: check that every layer has an owner and a metric","Interviews: say where on the stack a problem lives before you name a technology","Planning study: pick the chapters that match your role","Onboarding: give new engineers one picture of how the pieces connect"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A failure has several plausible causes in different layers","A proposal adds a layer such as an agent and nobody has shown the lower layers fall short","You must explain a system to non-specialists"],"avoid":["You are tuning one known parameter: go straight to that topic","You treat the map as a mandatory pipeline: many products need only two or three layers","The failing layer is already known and instrumented: go straight to that layer's topic"],"flips":["The corpus fits the context window and has one tenant: drop the knowledge layers and use the prompt","Steps vary at run time and need tools: add tools before you add agents","Regulation or per-user data is involved: access control and governance move from last to first"]},"alternatives":{"title":"How much of the stack to use","head":["Architecture","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Single prompt","Task is self-contained and the facts fit the window","Knowledge is large, changing or permissioned","Cheapest, but stale or oversized context"],["Prompt plus retrieval (RAG)","Large or changing corpus, citations, per-user access","Questions need whole-document reasoning or retrieval is poor","Ingestion, index and ACL work"],["Workflow with fixed code paths","Steps are known and must be predictable","Steps depend on what the model finds","Rigid, more code to maintain"],["Single agent with tools","Steps vary and tools return facts or take actions","Latency and cost budgets are tight","Loops, tool misuse, harder evals"],["Multi-agent","Work splits into independent specialists with separate context","One agent with good context would do","Coordination cost and compounding errors"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A team adds RAG to fix a style problem","cause":"The failing layer was not identified before choosing a fix","fix":"Name the symptom, find the layer in the map, then choose the cheapest fix at that layer"},{"symptom":"An agent is built for a task a workflow could do","cause":"Complexity was chosen before simpler layers were tested","fix":"Start with a prompt, add retrieval or tools only when evals show a gap"},{"symptom":"Security is bolted on last","cause":"Access control sits in the knowledge layers, not in the model call","fix":"Decide the permission model at ingestion, when ACLs are captured, not after launch"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w00-t02-s1","title":"The policy answer is out of date","situation":"Users report that the assistant quotes an old policy. The document was updated yesterday. A trace shows the retrieval step returned the old chunk.","constraints":["The assistant answers from a search index","The index returned the previous version of the text","Model and prompt are unchanged"],"unknowns":["How does new content reach the index?","Are old chunks deleted when a document changes?"],"question":"Where do you investigate first?","options":[{"name":"Switch to a larger model","verdict":"poor","why":"The model faithfully used the old chunk. A larger model cannot fix missing fresh data.","tradeoff":"A larger model helps when the right chunk is present but the model misreads it."},{"name":"The ingestion and update path (w03)","verdict":"best","why":"The index holds old text, so the update or delete path failed before retrieval or the model were involved.","tradeoff":"You must instrument sync lag and deletes to see the cause."},{"name":"Response and semantic caches (w02)","verdict":"good","why":"A cache can also serve a stale answer, so rule it out second.","tradeoff":"The trace already shows old retrieval output, so this is less likely the root cause."}],"flips":[{"if":"The trace shows the new chunk was retrieved but the answer is old","then":"Look at caches (w02) and then prompt assembly."},{"if":"Retrieval returns both versions","then":"Fix deletes and versioning in ingestion, and add a freshness filter."}],"takeaway":"Locate the layer with the trace, then fix at that layer."},{"id":"w00-t02-s2","title":"How much machinery for a small knowledge base","situation":"A team plans a multi-agent assistant for 40 stable internal pages (about 60,000 tokens) used by one department. Risk is low and budget is small.","constraints":["Corpus is small and rarely changes","Single tenant, no per-user permissions","Low risk, low budget"],"unknowns":["How often do pages change?","What accuracy does a baseline reach on a golden set?"],"question":"What architecture do you propose first?","options":[{"name":"RAG with hybrid search and a reranker","verdict":"good","why":"Works and scales when the corpus grows.","tradeoff":"Adds ingestion, an index and retrieval tuning that the constraints do not need."},{"name":"Multi-agent system with a retrieval agent and a writer agent","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds coordination cost and failure modes for a problem one call can solve.","tradeoff":"Multi-agent fits independent subtasks with large, separate contexts."},{"name":"Single prompt with the pages, plus prompt caching","verdict":"best","why":"The corpus fits the window, changes rarely and has one audience, so the simplest layer meets every constraint.","tradeoff":"It stops scaling if the corpus grows past a comfortable window or permissions appear."}],"flips":[{"if":"The corpus grows past what fits in the window or accuracy drops deep in context","then":"Move to RAG and measure retrieval quality."},{"if":"Different users may see different pages","then":"Choose retrieval with per-user access control."}],"takeaway":"Climb the stack only when a measured gap forces it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w00-t02-q1","type":"match","tag":"diagnose","q":"Match each symptom to the chapter that owns it.","pairs":[{"l":"A parameter is rejected with HTTP 400","r":"w01 LLM controls and APIs"},{"l":"First token arrives after many seconds","r":"w02 Latency and caching"},{"l":"Relevant chunk exists but is never returned","r":"w05 Retrieval"},{"l":"A user sees another team's document","r":"w07 Security and ACLs"}],"explain":"Parameter rules belong to the model call chapter, slow first tokens to latency and caching, missed chunks to retrieval and leaks across users to access control."},{"id":"w00-t02-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A task fits comfortably in the context window and the facts rarely change. What does the system map suggest first?","options":["A multi-agent design","A fine-tuned model","A vector database","A single prompt, adding layers only if evals fail"],"answer":3,"explain":"Use the least machinery that meets the constraints. Agents, fine-tuning and vector stores each add cost that the stated dials do not justify."},{"id":"w00-t02-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which concerns apply across every layer of the stack?","options":["Evaluation and observability","Cost and reliability","Governance and oversight","The temperature setting"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Proof, operations and governance apply to all layers. Temperature is a single model-call control in w01."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Name the nine stops a question passes through.","back":"Budget and API, latency and cache, knowledge, tools and protocols, agents, then senses, proof and operations around the answer."},{"front":"What is the default rule for how much of the stack to use?","back":"Use the simplest layer that meets the constraints, and add retrieval, tools or agents only when a measured gap appears."},{"front":"Where does access control get decided?","back":"At ingestion, when ACLs are captured, and at query time through filters. It is not a late add-on."}],"related":["w00-t03","w00-l1","w01-t14","w05-t01"],"refs":["tr1","r23","r24"]},{"id":"w00-t03","order":3,"group":"Answering well","title":"The Dial-and-Defend answer method","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Five moves that turn a design question into a defensible answer: clarify the dials, list options, pick and flip, name failure modes with detection, then state metrics and rollout.","useIf":"You face an open design question and need a structure that shows judgement under time pressure.","avoidIf":"The question is a factual lookup with one right answer, such as a parameter name.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **dial** is a fact about the situation that changes the best answer: corpus size, update rate, permission model, tenancy, latency target, risk and cost. Interviewers leave dials unspecified on purpose. A strong answer finds them before naming a technology, then defends a choice **for those dials** and says which dial change would flip it."},{"t":"flow","title":"The five moves","direction":"v","steps":[{"title":"1. Clarify the dials","md":"Ask two to four questions that would change the answer. State assumptions for anything unanswered.","tag":"30 s"},{"title":"2. List the options","md":"Name every sensible option, including the boring baseline to beat.","tag":"45 s"},{"title":"3. Pick and flip","md":"Choose for the stated dials. Say which dial and threshold would flip the choice.","tag":"60 s"},{"title":"4. Failure modes","md":"For the choice, name how it fails and how you would detect it.","tag":"60 s"},{"title":"5. Metrics and rollout","md":"State what you will measure, the threshold that counts as good, and how you ship safely.","tag":"45 s"}]},{"t":"p","md":"**Worked example.** Prompt: make an assistant answer questions about information the model never saw. Dials: the corpus is 80,000 tokens, changes monthly, one tenant, p95 latency under 4 seconds, low risk. Options: prompt with the documents (the boring baseline), RAG, fine-tuning. Pick the prompt with caching. Flip: if the corpus outgrows the window or permissions become per user, move to RAG. Failure: answers degrade deep in a long prompt, detected by a golden set with answers placed at different depths. Metrics: groundedness, p95 latency, cost per answered question. Rollout: shadow traffic, then a small cohort."},{"t":"table","caption":"The dial checklist","head":["Dial","Question to ask","Why it moves the answer"],"rows":[["Corpus","How large, how structured?","Decides prompt, RAG or index design"],["Update rate","How often does it change?","Decides ingestion and cache lifetime"],["Permissions","Who may see what, and how fast does revocation need to apply?","Decides filters, indexes and live checks"],["Tenancy","One tenant or many?","Decides isolation and cache keys"],["Latency","What p95 does the user feel?","Decides streaming, caches and model size"],["Risk","What does a wrong answer cost?","Decides guardrails and human review"],["Cost","What is the budget per task?","Decides routing and model choice"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"Say the flip aloud","md":"The sentence that separates good from great is the flip: \"If the corpus outgrows the window, or permissions become per user, I switch to RAG.\" It names a dial and a threshold, so the interviewer can test it."}],"whereToUse":["Design interviews for AI engineer and architect roles","Design reviews, where it keeps debate on constraints instead of favourite tools","Written proposals: each section maps to one move","Coaching sessions, where a facilitator scores answers against the five moves","Boss rounds in this guide, which use the same rubric"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The question names a goal but not the constraints","Several options are plausible and the choice depends on facts you can ask for","You have 3 to 10 minutes to speak","You need to show that you can change your mind for good reasons"],"avoid":["The interviewer asks for one specific fact, so answer it first and then add the method","You already have all the dials stated, so skip move 1 and say so","The prompt is a coding exercise with one correct output"],"flips":["Time is 90 seconds: keep one clarifying question, two options, one flip and one failure mode","Risk is high: spend more of the time on failure modes, detection and human review","The interviewer pushes back with a new constraint: restate it as a dial change and re-run moves 2 to 3"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Answer styles compared","head":["Style","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Dial-and-Defend","The question is open and trade-offs decide the answer","A single fact is asked","Needs practice to stay within the timebox"],["Name the technology","The interviewer wants a quick pointer","The context matters","Sounds confident, hides that you did not check constraints"],["Recite every technique","A survey question asks for the landscape","A decision is required","No judgement shown, long and unfocused"],["Whiteboard the architecture first","You are given time for diagrams","Dials are unknown","Draws a design that may not fit the constraints"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The answer starts with a product name","cause":"The candidate skipped the clarify step","fix":"Open with two or three questions about the dials, then state assumptions"},{"symptom":"The pick has no flip condition","cause":"The candidate treated the choice as universal","fix":"End the pick with: if dial X crosses threshold Y, I switch to Z"},{"symptom":"Failure modes are generic, such as hallucination","cause":"No link between the chosen option and how it breaks","fix":"Tie each failure to the option, and add how you would detect it, for example a golden set or a trace alert"},{"symptom":"The answer ends at the architecture","cause":"Metrics and rollout were treated as optional","fix":"Close with metrics, thresholds, shadow traffic and a rollback trigger"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w00-t03-s1","title":"The open design prompt","situation":"An interviewer says: design an assistant that helps support agents answer customer questions. You have 5 minutes and no other detail.","constraints":["Five minute timebox","Dials are unspecified","The interviewer will answer a few questions"],"unknowns":["How large and how fast-changing is the knowledge?","Who may see which content?","What is the latency target and the cost of a wrong answer?"],"question":"How do you open?","options":[{"name":"State assumptions without asking","verdict":"good","why":"Fast, and acceptable when the interviewer declines to answer.","tradeoff":"You may assume a dial wrongly and defend the wrong design."},{"name":"Ask 3 targeted dial questions, state assumptions for the rest, then list options with a baseline","verdict":"best","why":"It shows that the answer depends on facts and it fits the timebox.","tradeoff":"Uses about 30 seconds that a rival answer would spend on design."},{"name":"Start by naming a managed search service and a vector index","verdict":"poor","why":"It commits to RAG before knowing size, permissions or latency, so it may be the wrong layer.","tradeoff":"Naming a stack is right once the dials are known and RAG is the pick."}],"flips":[{"if":"The interviewer says the knowledge is a 30-page handbook","then":"Offer a prompt with the handbook as the baseline and show when RAG would replace it."},{"if":"The interviewer says each agent sees only their region's data","then":"Lead with permission filters and per-user access in the options."}],"takeaway":"Dials first, technology second."},{"id":"w00-t03-s2","title":"High-risk answer in four minutes","situation":"The prompt is a loan-advice assistant. Wrong answers can cause financial harm and the interviewer says you have four minutes.","constraints":["High risk","Four minute timebox","Regulated domain"],"unknowns":["Is a human in the loop before advice reaches the customer?","What evidence must be kept?"],"question":"How do you spend the time?","options":[{"name":"Spend three minutes on retrieval design","verdict":"poor","why":"Retrieval quality matters, but the stated risk puts safety and oversight first.","tradeoff":"Deep retrieval design is right when risk is low and the question is about search quality."},{"name":"Compress moves 1 to 3 into half the time, spend the rest on failure modes, detection, human review and rollout","verdict":"best","why":"In a high-risk domain the interviewer listens for how it fails and how you catch it.","tradeoff":"The option comparison is shorter, so name the boring baseline in one sentence."},{"name":"Give equal time to all five moves","verdict":"good","why":"Balanced and safe.","tradeoff":"Under-weights the high-risk dial, which should earn more time."}],"flips":[{"if":"Risk is low and the topic is internal search","then":"Shift time toward options and metrics."},{"if":"The interviewer asks to go deeper on one move","then":"Drop the others to one sentence each and go deep there."}],"takeaway":"Weight your time by the dial that carries the most risk."},{"id":"w00-t03-s3","title":"The interviewer changes a constraint","situation":"You chose a prompt with cached documents. The interviewer says the corpus is ten times larger and each user sees only some documents.","constraints":["The new facts are a size change and a permission change","You must respond in under a minute"],"unknowns":["What does a user's permission set look like?","How fast do permissions change?"],"question":"What do you say?","options":[{"name":"Throw away the design and start again from the beginning","verdict":"good","why":"Safe, but slow.","tradeoff":"It wastes the baseline, metrics and failure thinking you already did."},{"name":"Defend the prompt design with a larger window","verdict":"poor","why":"Per-user permissions and size break the single prompt design.","tradeoff":"Defending your pick is right only when the new fact does not cross your stated threshold."},{"name":"Restate both as dial changes, switch to RAG with access filters, and explain what you keep","verdict":"best","why":"You show that you reason from dials and that your earlier flip condition predicted this.","tradeoff":"You must be ready to cover ingestion and ACL capture next."}],"flips":[{"if":"The new facts do not cross your stated flip threshold","then":"Keep the choice and show the arithmetic."},{"if":"Revocation must apply in seconds","then":"Add a live permission check on sensitive hits."}],"takeaway":"Treat pushback as a dial change, not as an attack."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w00-t03-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the five moves in order.","items":["Clarify the dials","List the options","Pick and say what would flip it","Name failure modes and how to detect them","State metrics and rollout"],"explain":"You cannot list good options before the dials are known, and you cannot name failure modes before you pick an option. Metrics and rollout close the answer."},{"id":"w00-t03-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which sentence is a proper flip condition?","options":["If the corpus outgrows the window or permissions become per user, I switch to RAG","RAG is generally better","I would also consider fine-tuning","It depends on the use case"],"answer":0,"explain":"A flip names a dial and a threshold and the option you switch to. The other statements are opinions, an extra option or a hedge with no condition."},{"id":"w00-t03-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are dials you would ask about for a new assistant?","options":["Update rate of the content","Permission model","The colour of the user interface","Latency target at p95"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Update rate, permissions and latency each change which option wins. The interface colour does not affect the architecture choice."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"List the five moves of Dial-and-Defend.","back":"Clarify the dials, list the options (with the boring baseline), pick and flip, name failure modes with detection, then metrics and rollout."},{"front":"What makes a flip condition good?","back":"It names a dial, a threshold and the option you switch to, for example: if permissions become per user, I move to RAG."},{"front":"Name the seven dials in the checklist.","back":"Corpus, update rate, permissions, tenancy, latency, risk and cost."}],"related":["w00-t04","w00-l3","w00-l6","w01-t14"],"refs":["r26","r27","tr1"]},{"id":"w00-t04","order":4,"group":"Answering well","title":"Reading and answering scenario questions","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A scenario card gives a few fixed constraints and hides the rest. You choose an option, then check its verdict, reason and trade-off against the constraints.","useIf":"You practise with scenario cards and want to learn from the verdicts, not only collect a score.","avoidIf":"You want a single memorised answer per card. Verdicts change when the dials change.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Every scenario card has the same parts. The **situation** gives facts, and facts it gives are real. The **constraints** list the dial values that are fixed in this card. The **unknowns** are questions a strong candidate would ask, and they mark dials you must not assume. The **options** each carry a verdict, a reason and a trade-off. After you choose, you see all of them, plus the **flips**: changed constraints that would change the winner."},{"t":"flow","title":"How to read a card","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Mark constraints","md":"Underline every fixed dial value."},{"title":"Read the unknowns","md":"Do not assume them. Note which could change your pick."},{"title":"Cross out","md":"Drop options that break a stated constraint."},{"title":"Pick by cost of error","md":"Among the rest, choose the one that is cheapest to be wrong about."},{"title":"Check the flips","md":"Ask what you would change if one flip happened."}]},{"t":"p","md":"The card is **answerable from the stated constraints**. If you need a fact it does not give, that fact is an unknown, and the right move is to say how the answer would change, not to invent a value. Choose in two passes. First eliminate: an option that breaks a stated constraint is out, whatever its reputation. Then compare the survivors by the cost of being wrong, including how hard each is to reverse. Write one sentence of reasoning before you reveal, because the reveal is only useful if you can compare it with your own logic."},{"t":"table","caption":"What the verdicts mean","head":["Verdict","Meaning","How to use it when revising"],"rows":[["best","Fits every stated constraint at an acceptable cost. Exactly one per card.","Learn the constraint that made it win and its stated trade-off."],["good","Acceptable, with a cost the best option avoids.","Know the cost, because a flip can promote it to best."],["poor","Fails a stated constraint or adds cost the constraints do not need.","Read where it would be right, because it is usually best somewhere else."]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Poor options are not wrong options","md":"Every poor option explains the situation where it would win. Revise those sentences: they are your flip conditions for the interview."}],"whereToUse":["Every topic page in this guide ends with scenario cards that follow this format","Group sessions, where pairs commit to an option before the reveal","Self-testing: write your reason first, then compare it with the card's reason","Interview practice: turn the card's constraints into the clarifying questions you would ask"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You can name the constraint that rules out at least one option","You want to practise trade-off language such as 'this costs us X'","You review a topic and need a quick test of whether you understood the dials"],"avoid":["You are guessing: a lucky pick teaches nothing, so write your reason before you reveal","You carry a favourite tool into every card: the card tests the constraints, not the tool","You only want the score: the value sits in the reasons and the flips, which the score hides"],"flips":["You consistently pick the sophisticated option: re-read the cost column of each card","You always pick the cheap one: look for the constraint, such as per-user permissions, that rules it out","A card feels unanswerable: read the unknowns, they list what the card deliberately leaves open"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to practise a trade-off","head":["Method","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Scenario card with verdicts","You need fast feedback on constraint reading","You need to practise speaking","Can become a guessing game if you skip your own reason"],["Dial board","You want to see how the ranking moves when one dial changes","You need a single committed answer","Scores are authored judgements, not measurements"],["Boss round with rubric","You want a full spoken answer scored on structure","You have under 10 minutes","Needs time and honest self-scoring"],["Quiz item","A fact or mechanism has one right answer","The right answer depends on dials","Tests recall more than judgement"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"You choose an option and then explain why the card is wrong","cause":"You added an assumption the card did not state","fix":"Reread the constraints and the unknowns, and treat unstated facts as open questions"},{"symptom":"You treat 'good' as a wrong answer","cause":"You read verdicts as a score instead of a cost statement","fix":"Read the trade-off of the good option: it names the cost, and a flip can make it the best"},{"symptom":"You pick the most advanced option every time","cause":"Sophistication feels safer","fix":"Check whether a stated constraint needs it, and prefer the cheapest option that meets every constraint"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w00-t04-s1","title":"Small stable corpus, tight latency","situation":"A team needs an assistant over about 150,000 tokens of documentation. Answers must arrive within 3 seconds at p95.","constraints":["One tenant, no per-user permissions","Content changes monthly","p95 latency of 3 seconds","Low risk"],"unknowns":["How much accuracy drops deep inside a long prompt on this model?","How many questions per day reuse the same prefix?"],"question":"Which option fits the stated constraints?","options":[{"name":"RAG with hybrid search","verdict":"good","why":"Scales and gives citations.","tradeoff":"Adds ingestion, an index and retrieval tuning for a corpus that fits the window."},{"name":"Fine-tune a model on the documentation","verdict":"poor","why":"Studies found retrieval beats fine-tuning for injecting knowledge [[r27]].","tradeoff":"Fine-tuning is right for tone, format or a smaller model, not for facts."},{"name":"Put the documents in the prompt and use prompt caching","verdict":"best","why":"The corpus is under the roughly 200,000 token level Anthropic suggests can go straight into a prompt [[r26]], it is stable, and a cached prefix keeps latency and cost down.","tradeoff":"Accuracy can fall for facts buried deep in a long input, so test with a golden set at different depths."}],"flips":[{"if":"The corpus grows to several million tokens","then":"RAG wins, because the corpus no longer fits."},{"if":"Each user may see only some documents","then":"Choose retrieval with access filters."}],"takeaway":"Constraints rule options out, then cost picks among the rest."},{"id":"w00-t04-s2","title":"Two options fit every constraint","situation":"On a card, two options both satisfy every stated constraint. One is simpler and easy to reverse. The other is more capable but needs new infrastructure.","constraints":["Both options meet the stated dials","The team is small","Requirements are expected to change"],"unknowns":["Which unstated dial would favour the more capable option?"],"question":"How do you choose and justify?","options":[{"name":"Ask for more information and decide after the answer","verdict":"good","why":"Reasonable in a live conversation.","tradeoff":"On a card or with a time limit you must still commit and state your assumption."},{"name":"Choose the simpler, reversible option, and name the unknown that would flip you","verdict":"best","why":"Cost of being wrong is lowest, and naming the flip shows you know when the other option wins.","tradeoff":"If the flip happens you will migrate later."},{"name":"Choose the more capable option because it covers more cases","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds infrastructure that no stated constraint needs.","tradeoff":"It is right once an unstated dial, such as rapid growth, is confirmed."}],"flips":[{"if":"Requirements are fixed and the capable option has a known payoff","then":"The capable option wins."},{"if":"The team has already run the infrastructure for other products","then":"The cost gap narrows and the capable option can win."}],"takeaway":"When two fit, take the cheaper to reverse and name the flip."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w00-t04-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does a 'good' verdict mean on a scenario card?","options":["The option is wrong but close","The option is the best in another situation","The option scores half a point","The option is acceptable, with a cost the best option avoids"],"answer":3,"explain":"Good means acceptable with a cost. A poor option is the one that fails a constraint and states where it would be right. Verdicts are not scores."},{"id":"w00-t04-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the steps for reading a card.","items":["Mark the fixed constraints","Read the unknowns without assuming them","Cross out options that break a constraint","Pick the cheapest option to be wrong about","Check the flips"],"explain":"Constraints come first because they eliminate options. Cost of error decides among the survivors, and the flips test your pick."},{"id":"w00-t04-q3","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Which habits make you misread scenario cards?","options":["Adding facts the card did not state","Choosing the most sophisticated option by default","Writing your reason before the reveal","Ignoring the unknowns list"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Assumptions, a bias toward sophistication and skipping the unknowns all cause misreads. Writing your reason first is the habit that fixes them."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What do the constraints on a scenario card tell you?","back":"Which dials are fixed in this card. Facts not listed are open, and the unknowns name the questions you would ask."},{"front":"How do you use a poor option when revising?","back":"Read where it would be right. That sentence is a flip condition you can say in an interview."},{"front":"Two options both fit every constraint. What do you do?","back":"Pick the cheaper one to reverse and name the unknown that would flip you to the other."}],"related":["w00-t03","w00-t01","w00-l2","w01-t14"],"refs":["r26","r27"]},{"id":"w00-t05","order":5,"group":"Freshness","title":"What changed in 2025 to 2026","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A dated map of the API, protocol and platform changes that break code or old advice, with GA, Preview, Deprecated or Retired status checked on 2026-10-13.","useIf":"You read an older tutorial, answer an interview question about a platform, or plan an upgrade.","avoidIf":"You need today's price or limit: confirm it in the live documentation, because this map is dated.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Most of this map is about **old advice that now misleads**: a tutorial teaches Chat Completions plus reasoning plus tools, an answer says MCP needs `initialize` and a session id, or a design uses an API that has been shut down. Status values were checked on **2026-10-13**. **GA** means generally available, **Preview** has no SLA and can change, **Deprecated** still works but has an end date, and **Retired** has stopped working. When a source gave no explicit status, the row says so."},{"t":"p","md":"Three patterns repeat across the rows. **Parameters get stricter**: newer models reject sampling values, forced tool choice or an effort value that older models accepted. **State and protocols move toward explicit handles**: Responses and Conversations hold state on the server, and MCP dropped its implicit session. **Old surfaces end on a date**: Assistants, On Your Data, preview hosted agents and several model snapshots. Sort your own estate by the earliest end date first."},{"t":"h","level":3,"text":"Models and APIs","id":"models-and-apis"},{"t":"table","head":["Date","Change","Status","Source"],"compact":true,"rows":[["2025-03-11","Responses API released with built-in tools","GA","[[r1]]"],["2025-08","Azure OpenAI v1 API opt-in: base URL `/openai/v1`, no `api-version`","GA","[[r28]]"],["2025-08-20","Conversations API released. It is not ZDR eligible","GA","[[r29]]"],["2025-08-26","Assistants API deprecation announced, sunset 2026-08-26. Azure status after that date is unverified","Retired on OpenAI","[[r4]] [[r2]]"],["2026-03-05","GPT-5.4: Chat Completions rejects tools when reasoning effort is not `none`","GA","[[r3]]"],["2026-04-16","Claude Opus 4.7: new tokenizer (about 30% more tokens), non-default sampling values return 400","GA","[[r7]]"],["2026-09-03","GPT-6 Astra: async tool calling, and `reasoning.effort: none` returns HTTP 400 on Astra","GA","[[r30]]"],["2026-09-22 and 2026-09-28","Claude Opus 5.5 (thinking cannot be disabled) and Sonnet 5.5 (forced `tool_choice` returns 400)","GA","[[r7]]"],["2026-09-29","GPT-6.1 Sol: Chat Completions works only without tools","GA","[[r30]]"]]},{"t":"h","level":3,"text":"Caching, cost and tuning","id":"caching-cost-and-tuning"},{"t":"table","head":["Date","Change","Status","Source"],"compact":true,"rows":[["2025-12-11, 2026-02-10","`/responses/compact`, then server-side compaction with `context_management`","GA","[[r1]]"],["2026-07-09 (model version date)","GPT-5.6 caching: cache writes billed (1.25x input on OpenAI), explicit breakpoints, `prompt_cache_options`. Older models return 400 for the new fields","GA","[[r5]] [[r6]]"],["2026-09-08","Prompt Cache Diagnostics for GPT-5.6 and later in Responses","GA","[[r1]]"],["2026-05-07","OpenAI winds down self-serve fine-tuning. No new jobs for active customers after 2027-01-06","Deprecated","[[r2]]"],["2026-06-03","Evals platform, reusable prompts and Agent Builder deprecated. Evals read-only 2026-10-31, all shut down 2026-11-30","Deprecated","[[r2]]"],["2026-09-25","Azure Flex request to a model without Flex returns 400, not a fallback","Flex is Preview","[[r31]]"]]},{"t":"h","level":3,"text":"Protocols","id":"protocols"},{"t":"table","head":["Date","Change","Status","Source"],"compact":true,"rows":[["2025-03-26","MCP: Streamable HTTP replaces HTTP+SSE, OAuth 2.1 framework added","Spec revision","[[tr2]]"],["2025-06-18","MCP servers become OAuth resource servers, RFC 8707 resource indicators required of clients, batching removed","Spec revision","[[tr3]]"],["2025-11-25, 2025-12-09","MCP: URL elicitation and experimental Tasks. Then MCP moves under the Agentic AI Foundation","Spec revision","[[tr4]] [[r9]]"],["2026-03-12","A2A v1.0.0 with breaking renames. v1.0.1 followed on 2026-05-28","GA","[[r10]] [[r32]]"],["2026-07-28","MCP: stateless protocol (no `initialize`, no session id), multi round-trip requests replace server push, sampling and roots deprecated, Tasks become an extension, deterministic `tools/list`","Spec revision","[[r8]]"],["2026-10-13","Foundry serves A2A preview 0.3 unless the version header or query value requests 1.0","Check header","[[r11]]"]]},{"t":"h","level":3,"text":"Agent platforms, search and policy","id":"agent-platforms-search-and-policy"},{"t":"table","head":["Date","Change","Status","Source"],"compact":true,"rows":[["2026-04-03","Microsoft Agent Framework 1.0 announced. Core is stable, hosting, A2A, DevUI and AG-UI packages stay preview","GA core","[[r12]]"],["2026-05-01","Microsoft Agent 365 GA (per user). Team-workflow agents with their own access were public preview at launch","GA","[[r15]]"],["2026-08-20","Foundry initial-preview hosted agent backend support ends. Redeploy on the session-sandbox model","Deprecated","[[r33]]"],["2026-04-01","AI Search stable API: index projections and Content Understanding skill GA, first stable agentic retrieval is `minimal` extractive only","GA","[[r16]] [[r17]]"],["2026-10-14","Azure OpenAI On Your Data retires, one day after the check date","Deprecated","[[r19]]"],["2026-07-27","EU Digital Omnibus on AI in force. Annex III high-risk moves to 2027-12-02, Annex I to 2028-08-02","In force","[[r22]]"]]},{"t":"h","level":3,"text":"Dates to pin","id":"dates-to-pin"},{"t":"table","head":["Date","What happens","Platform"],"compact":true,"rows":[["2026-10-23","Shutdown of `gpt-4`, `gpt-4-turbo`, `gpt-3.5-turbo`, `o1`, `o3-mini`, `o4-mini`, `gpt-4.1-nano` and related fine-tunes","OpenAI API [[r2]]"],["2026-10-31 and 2026-11-30","Evals read-only, then shutdown of Evals, reusable prompts and Agent Builder","OpenAI API [[r2]]"],["2026-11-19","`o1`, `o3`, `o4-mini` and related retire","Azure OpenAI [[r18]]"],["2026-12-01","Foundry Workflows (preview) retire","Foundry [[r25]]"],["2026-12-09","`gpt-4o` version 2024-05-13 retires","Azure OpenAI [[r18]]"],["2027-04-14","The `gpt-4.1` family retires","Azure OpenAI [[r18]]"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Platform dates differ","md":"The same model can retire on different days on OpenAI and Azure, for example `gpt-5` on 2026-12-11 on the OpenAI API against 2027-02-09 on Azure. Always name the platform when you quote a date."}],"whereToUse":["Before answering an interview question about a specific API or protocol version","When reviewing a tutorial or a code sample that is more than a few months old","When planning upgrades, to sort work by the earliest end date","When writing a design document that cites a product feature and needs a status label"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A source predates 2026-07 and teaches Assistants, HTTP+SSE or Chat Completions with reasoning tools","You quote a feature as available and must say GA or Preview","You own a dated shutdown list and need to sequence migrations"],"avoid":["You need current prices or quotas: this map does not carry them","You assume one platform's date applies to another","You treat a vendor announcement date as the date a feature is available in your region or tier"],"flips":["A row's status changes after 2026-10-13: trust the live page and update your notes","The answer will be recorded in a design document: cite the source link and the check date","You use a preview feature in production: record the fallback and the review date"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to keep up with change","head":["Method","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Dated freshness map (this topic)","You need a fast list of what broke and what ends","Weeks pass after the check date","Goes stale, so read the check date"],["Vendor changelogs and deprecation pages","You need the authoritative date","You need synthesis across vendors","Long and vendor-shaped"],["Spec revisions and release notes","A protocol or SDK is in your stack","You only need a summary","Detailed, and different pages can disagree"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Chat Completions agent code fails after a model upgrade","cause":"From GPT-5.4, tools with reasoning effort other than `none` need Responses, and a default effort of `medium` still counts","fix":"Move the tool loop to Responses, or set effort to `none` and accept the loss of reasoning"},{"symptom":"An MCP client waits for a session id that never arrives","cause":"The 2026-07-28 revision removed `initialize` and the session id","fix":"Read the revision your server speaks and negotiate the version"},{"symptom":"A GA claim fails a review","cause":"A product can be GA at the API level while a portal or a package is Preview","fix":"State which surface you mean, for example API GA and portal Preview"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w00-t05-s1","title":"Agent code breaks after a model upgrade","situation":"A Chat Completions agent that calls tools ran on an older reasoning model. After moving the Azure deployment to `gpt-5.6-sol` it fails with 'Function tools with reasoning_effort are not supported', although the code never sets an effort.","constraints":["Tool calling is required","The model must keep reasoning for hard questions","A release is due this month"],"unknowns":["How many tool loops depend on Chat Completions message shapes?","Is Responses available in the same Azure region?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Keep Chat Completions and set `reasoning_effort` to `none`","verdict":"good","why":"The tool loop runs unchanged, which fits a tight release.","tradeoff":"You give up reasoning, so hard questions get worse."},{"name":"Move the agent loop to the Responses API","verdict":"best","why":"Tools with reasoning need Responses on these models, and it keeps reasoning on [[r3]] [[tr5]].","tradeoff":"You rewrite message handling for Items and re-test the tool loop."},{"name":"Keep the code and omit the effort parameter","verdict":"poor","why":"The default effort is `medium`, so the model still reasons and the call still fails.","tradeoff":"Omitting a parameter helps only when the default is `none`, which holds for some earlier models, so check the model page."}],"flips":[{"if":"Quality tests show reasoning adds nothing for this task","then":"Chat Completions with effort `none` is cheaper to ship."},{"if":"The code must stay portable across OpenAI-compatible vendors","then":"Keep Chat Completions with effort `none` behind an adapter and isolate the Responses path."}],"takeaway":"An unset parameter takes the model's default, and the default may be the problem."},{"id":"w00-t05-s2","title":"Sequence the migrations by date","situation":"On 2026-10-13 a team has a workload on `o4-mini` through the OpenAI API, a Foundry deployment of `gpt-4o` 2024-05-13, and eval dashboards on the OpenAI Evals platform.","constraints":["Two engineers are available","Each migration needs regression tests","Dates differ by platform"],"unknowns":["Can the `o4-mini` workload move to a current model without prompt rewrites?","Which eval datasets must be exported?"],"question":"Which work goes first?","options":[{"name":"Start with the Azure `gpt-4o` migration because it is the largest","verdict":"poor","why":"Size is not urgency. The 2026-10-23 and 2026-10-31 dates arrive first.","tradeoff":"Starting with the largest item is right when its date is also the earliest."},{"name":"Migrate everything in one release in December","verdict":"good","why":"One test cycle and one rollout.","tradeoff":"It misses the October dates and puts all the risk in one release."},{"name":"Replace `o4-mini` before 2026-10-23, export Evals before 2026-10-31, then handle the Azure `gpt-4o` date of 2026-12-09","verdict":"best","why":"It sorts the work by the earliest end date, and the OpenAI shutdown is only 10 days away [[r2]].","tradeoff":"The Azure work waits and has a shorter runway."}],"flips":[{"if":"The `o4-mini` workload already runs on another model","then":"The Evals export and the Azure date become the first items."},{"if":"OpenAI extends a shutdown date","then":"Re-sort the list, but keep the earliest published date until it is moved."}],"takeaway":"Sort migrations by end date per platform, not by size."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w00-t05-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each date to the change.","pairs":[{"l":"2025-03-11","r":"Responses API released"},{"l":"2026-03-12","r":"A2A v1.0.0 published"},{"l":"2026-07-28","r":"MCP revision removes `initialize` and the session id"},{"l":"2026-08-26","r":"OpenAI Assistants API sunset"}],"explain":"These four dates anchor the API, protocol and retirement changes. The map above gives the source for each."},{"id":"w00-t05-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A client talks to Foundry A2A with no version header or query value. Which protocol version does it get?","options":["1.0","1.0.1","Preview 0.3","The newest preview"],"answer":2,"explain":"On the check date Foundry served preview 0.3 unless the header or the query value requested another version [[r11]]. The header and the query value must agree or the call returns 400."},{"id":"w00-t05-q3","type":"multi","tag":"bug","q":"Which changes can break existing code without any change on your side?","options":["Chat Completions rejecting tools with reasoning effort other than `none` from GPT-5.4","Claude Opus 4.7 returning 400 for non-default sampling values","A model snapshot reaching its shutdown date","A new reference page in the docs"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"A parameter rule, a sampling rule and a shutdown each change behaviour on a date. A new documentation page changes nothing at run time."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What happened to the OpenAI Assistants API?","back":"Deprecation was announced on 2025-08-26 and the API was sunset on 2026-08-26. Threads map to Conversations and Runs to Responses."},{"front":"What changed in MCP on 2026-07-28?","back":"The protocol became stateless: no `initialize` and no session id, multi round-trip requests replaced server push, and Tasks moved to an extension."},{"front":"Why can an unset reasoning effort still break tool calls?","back":"The model applies its default, often `medium`, and Chat Completions rejects tools with any effort other than `none` on newer models."},{"front":"Why name the platform when quoting a retirement date?","back":"The same model can retire on different dates on OpenAI and on Azure."}],"related":["w00-l4","w00-l5","w01-t12","w01-t03","w08-t01"],"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r7","r8","r10","r11","r12","r15","r18","r25","r30","r33","tr2","tr3","tr4","tr5"]}]});
